<h2 id=introduction>
Introduction</h2>

<em>This section is non-normative.</em>

The Aqueous archive format stores a directory tree, or a single file, as a sequence of blocks.
This section gives an overview of how the parts of the format fit together;
each paragraph points to the chapter defining the part it describes.

An archive is a header, a sequence of blocks and a footer ([[#container]]).
Every block starts with its type and its size, so a decompressor can skip any block without understanding it,
and the type tells whether the block can be ignored when it is not understood ([[#block-types]]).
The footer points back to the last metadata block, so that a decompressor can start reading from the end of the archive.

Blocks are grouped into <em>segments</em>, each ending with a metadata block ([[#segments]]).
The metadata block names the entries of the segment, i.e. directories, files, symbolic links and other nodes,
and points each file to the block holding its content.
A later segment adds, replaces and removes entries of earlier segments,
so that an archive can be extended by appending a segment without rewriting what precedes it.
An archive without any metadata block simply decompresses into a single unnamed file, as a gzip file does.

The content of a file is given by a single <em>leaf block</em>.
The simplest leaf block is a data block storing the content itself, optionally compressed with Zstandard
and a dictionary shared by many small blocks ([[#stored-data]]).
A formula block is a leaf block that describes the content instead,
as a small stack machine program over <em>non-leaf blocks</em> following it ([[#formulas]]).
The program takes slices of them, concatenates them, patches them,
and applies WebAssembly modules to them to undo a transformation made by the compressor ([[#modules]]).
A leaf block and the non-leaf blocks following it form a <em>parcel</em>,
which is typically all that a decompressor reads to extract a single file,
and the formula block serves as an index for random access within it.

An overlay archive describes a directory tree as changes to an existing one, called its base ([[#overlays]]).
Import blocks read byte ranges of files in the base, which formulas can patch,
so that an overlay archive works as an update or a delta of the base.

The whole archive may be encrypted with a passphrase using age [[AGE]] ([[#encrypted-archive]]).
Checksums cover every block except, optionally, leaf data blocks,
and except shorthand blocks, which name registered dictionaries and modules by their hashes ([[#registered-blocks]]).
Mirror blocks hold copies of the blocks whose damage would affect many files ([[#recovery]]).
Metadata that is not necessary to read the files, such as timestamps, comments and permissions,
is encoded in CBOR and may be ignored by a decompressor ([[#optional-metadata]], [[#extraction]]).

<h2 id=conventions>
Conventions</h2>

<h3 id=expressions>
Expressions</h3>

All expressions are interpreted according to the standard arithmetics, with the following extensions:

*   |a| / |b| equals floor(|a| ÷ |b|).
*   |a| % |b|, where |b| > 0, is a non-negative modulo of |a| divided by |b|, and equals |a| - (|a| / |b|) × |b|.
*   |a| &lt;&lt; |b| equals |a| × 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |a| &gt;&gt; |b| equals |a| / 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |XX|<sub>16</sub> denotes a hexadecimal integer.
*   (|a|, |b|, …) denotes an ordered array of values.
*   (|XX| |YY| |ZZ|)<sub>16</sub> is a shorthand for (|XX|<sub>16</sub>, |YY|<sub>16</sub>, |ZZ|<sub>16</sub>).
*   |a| || |b| concatenates two arrays |a| and |b|, treating any non-array value as a singleton array (|a|) or (|b|).
*   For array |a|, |a|<sup>|n|</sup> equals |a| || |a|<sup>|n|-1</sup> when |n| > 0.
    |a|<sup>0</sup> equals an empty array ().
*   [|a|, |b|] denotes a closed interval between |a| and |b|, both inclusive.
*   [|a|, |b|) denotes a half-open interval between |a| (inclusive) and |b| (exclusive).

All expressions result in an integer between -(2<sup>64</sup> - 1) and 2<sup>64</sup> - 1,
or an array of those integers with the number of elements not exceeding 2<sup>64</sup> - 1.
Note that subexpressions still may exceed this range.

<h3 id=types>
Types</h3>

Types are a set of values and associated encoding scheme.
A type or a structure is <dfn export>implicitly-sized</dfn> when its byte length is only available through the context.
Unless stated as being implicitly-sized,
all types have the defined byte length out of the context.

<dfn for=type>u8</dfn> is an unsigned 8-bit integer, occupying 1 byte.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>8</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u16le</dfn> is an unsigned 16-bit integer, occupying 2 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order (least significant byte first).
Its range is [0, 2<sup>16</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u32le</dfn> is an unsigned 32-bit integer, occupying 4 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>32</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u64le</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer, occupying 8 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong><dfn for=type>u</dfn>(|n|)</strong>, where |n| is an integer less than 2<sup>64</sup>,
is the smallest integer type that can represent |n| if |n| > 0,
and a zero-sized type if |n| = 0.
In other words,

* If |n| = 0, u(|n|) is zero-sized.
* If |n| ∈ [1, 2<sup>8</sup>), u(|n|) is the same as [=u8=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>8</sup>, 2<sup>16</sup>), u(|n|) is the same as [=u16le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>16</sup>, 2<sup>32</sup>), u(|n|) is the same as [=u32le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>32</sup>, 2<sup>64</sup>), u(|n|) is the same as [=u64le=].

<dfn for=type>leb128</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer encoded using the variable number of bytes with the LEB128 encoding.
Each byte, except the last, has its most significant bit (MSB) set to 1, indicating that more bytes follow.
The remaining 7 bits of each byte are used to store the integer's value.
The least significant 7 bits of the first byte are the least significant bits of the integer, and so on.
The last byte has its MSB set to 0.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>):
an encoding MUST be at most 10 bytes long, and MUST NOT encode a value of 2<sup>64</sup> or more.

<div class=note>
  This allows multiple encodings for any number less than 2<sup>63</sup>, padded with bytes of zero bits,
  unless the shortest encoding is required.
</div>

<strong>|a|[|n|]</strong>, where |n| is an integer, denotes an ordered array of *a*s.
|n|'s range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong>|a|[*]</strong> denotes an [=implicitly-sized=] ordered array of *a*s with an unspecified number of elements.
The number of elements is implicitly determined from the context.
This specification uses two kinds of such contexts:

*   When one or more |a|[*] fields are in the structure with the defined byte length,
    the numbers of elements in those fields are increased as needed to exactly fill the required byte length.
    There MUST be a single set of the numbers of elements that satisfy these conditions.
*   When an |a|[*] field is given with a constraint,
    the field extends until, but not including, the first instance of |a| which doesn't satisfy the required constraint.

<h3 id=structure>
Structure</h3>

**Structure** is a logical group of one or more ordered **fields** of possibly differing types concatenated together.
Each field has the following properties:

* The descriptive **name** of the field (e.g. [=block/size=], [=cdata/compression_params=]).
* The data **type** of the field (e.g. [=leb128=], [=u8=][[=block/size=]]), with an optional constraint.
* Optional criteria or **conditions** that dictate the presence, value, or interpretation of the field.

Field names can only coincide when the field's type varies by conditions,
in which case at most one such condition is applicable at any time.
The name can be omitted when the field's name is the same as the previous field's.

The field type may be <dfn for=type>offset</dfn>,
which is a special zero-sized type interpreted as
the offset to the following byte relative to the unspecified file position (origin).
Offsets are only used to measure the number of bytes within a set of fields.
The syntax and semantics of this specification don't depend on the choice of origin.

The optional constraint does not affect the field's encoding and only narrows down the possible set of values. Constraints can be one of the following:

*   |a| ∈ |X| requires that the value is in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| ∉ |X| requires that the value is not in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| = |x| requires that the value is exactly the given value |x|.
    If |x| is a constant expression, this effectively fixes the byte encoding of this field.
*   |a| ≠ |x| requires that the value is not the given value |x|.

The conditions may be one of the following:

* "<dfn for=cond>Optional</dfn>" indicates that conforming implementations MAY include or exclude given field as needed.
* "<dfn for=cond>If</dfn> …" indicates that the field MUST be included if and only if the specified condition is true.
* "<dfn for=cond>Depends on</dfn> …" indicates that the field's interpretation varies with the specified set of other fields.

Structures are [=implicitly-sized=] only when their byte length is not available through the context
and at least one [=implicitly-sized=] field exists therein.

