  and that of [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=] is also a prefix of [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRRORED_HEADER_MAGIC=].
</div>

<h3 id=semantics>
Semantics and failures</h3>

This specification defines the meaning of an archive as a function from a byte sequence to an [=archive value=],
which also takes a [=base=] for an [=overlay archive=].
Every algorithm in this specification is declarative:
it defines *what* the result is, not how or in which order it has to be computed,
and a conforming implementation MAY compute it in any other way that produces the same result.

The result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=] is either an [=archive value=] or a failure,
which is one of the following:

* An <dfn>invalid archive</dfn> failure occurs when the input violates any requirement of this specification.
    It is a property of the input alone, and all conforming decompressors agree on it.
* A <dfn>resource failure</dfn> occurs when the evaluation exceeds a declared or implementation-defined limit.
    It is not a property of the input, and conforming decompressors MAY disagree on it.
* A <dfn>decryption failure</dfn> occurs when an [[#encrypted-archive|encrypted archive]] can't be decrypted
    with the given passphrase, if any.
    It is a property of the input and the passphrase, and all conforming decompressors agree on it.
* A <dfn>base failure</dfn> occurs when an [=overlay archive=] is interpreted without a [=base=],
    or an [=import/import=] can't be resolved in the given base ([[#import-resolution]]).
    It is a property of the input and the base, and all conforming decompressors agree on it.
* An <dfn>unsupported failure</dfn> occurs when the input contains a block of a [=critical=] type
    not defined by this specification ([[#reserved-blocks]]),
    or a [=shorthand block=] whose [=registered hash=] the decompressor doesn't know ([[#registered-blocks]]).
    It is a property of the input, the version of this specification and the registry entries known,
    and all conforming decompressors implementing the same version and knowing the same entries agree on it.

<div class=note>
  An unsupported failure tells that a decompressor implementing a newer version may be able to interpret the input,
  which is not the case for an [=invalid archive=] failure in general.
  It takes precedence over an [=invalid archive=] failure in any block ([[#interpretation]]),
  since a future version may give a different meaning to the other blocks.
</div>

A conforming compressor, given an [=archive value=],
MUST produce a byte sequence whose interpretation is that [=archive value=].
When it produces an [=overlay archive=], it is also given a [=base=],
and the interpretation with that base MUST be that archive value.
This specification doesn't constrain how the compressor chooses blocks, block sizes, dictionaries or formulas.

<h2 id=container>
Container</h2>

<h3 id=block>
Block</h3>

**Blocks** are a typed byte sequence.
The entirety of an unencrypted archive and the beginning of an encrypted archive consist of either blocks or block-like structures.

Blocks have the following generic structure:

<div dfn-for=block>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>type</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ∉ {[=FOOTER_TYPE=], [=OVERLAY_FOOTER_TYPE=]}
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 0
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u16le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 1
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u32le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 2
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u64le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 3
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=block/type=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>padding</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>[=If=] ([=block_end=] - [=block_start=]) % 2 = 1
  </tbody>
</table>

Some [=block/type=] values, including [=FOOTER_TYPE=] and [=OVERLAY_FOOTER_TYPE=], are also used to denote block-like structures.
For example the archive footer has to have a distinct block type in order to be placed after blocks.
This specification doesn't assign any meaning to such block-like structures unless explicitly stated.

[=block/type=] MUST use the shortest [=leb128=] encoding.

The presence of [=block/padding=] ensures that there are even number of bytes in any block.
Conforming compressors MUST set [=block/padding=] to zero,
but its value doesn't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=].

<div class=note>
  [=block/padding=] carries no information,
  so its damage is not a reason to reject an otherwise readable archive.
  On the other hand, [=block/type=] determines the width of [=block/size=] and where the next block starts,
  so a single encoding for each type leaves no ambiguity in finding block boundaries, e.g. by scanning.
</div>

Later sections describe the contents of [=block/data=] field according to each defined [=block/type=].
Since [=block/type=] also determines the byte length of [=block/size=],
there are generally multiple [=block/type=] values assigned for each logical block type.

</div>

<h4 id=block-types>
Block types</h4>

The following table lists every range of block types.
A range of block types for a single logical block type is also described in binary,
e.g. <code>0001&thinsp;0|m||ss|</code>,
where letters denote bits that determine the parameters of the block.
|ss| always denotes the two least significant bits, which determine the width of [=block/size=] as above:
0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=] and 3 for [=u64le=].
Whether a reserved block type is [=critical=] is determined by its [=leading digit=] ([[#reserved-blocks]]).

Block types of 2 hexadecimal digits are as follows:

