      <td>[[#mirror-block]] for [[#dictionary-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>C58<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C5F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>No
      <td>[[#mirror-block]] for [[#import-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>C60<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C63<sub>16</sub>
      <td>No
      <td>[[#mirror-block]] for [[#module-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>C64<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C67<sub>16</sub>
      <td>No
      <td>[[#mirror-block]] for [[#resource-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>C68<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C6F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>No
      <td>Reserved
    <tr>
      <td>C70<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C77<sub>16</sub>
      <td>No
      <td>[[#mirror-block]] for [[#formula-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>C78<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;CFF<sub>16</sub>, C000<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;CFFF<sub>16</sub>, …
      <td>No
      <td>Reserved
    <tr>
      <td>D00<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;DFF<sub>16</sub>, D000<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;DFFF<sub>16</sub>, …
      <td>No
      <td>[=Reserved for development environments=]
    <tr>
      <td>E00<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;EFF<sub>16</sub>, E000<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;EFFF<sub>16</sub>, …
      <td>No
      <td>[=Reserved for experiments=]
    <tr>
      <td>F00<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;FFF<sub>16</sub>, F000<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;FFFF<sub>16</sub>, …
      <td>No
      <td>Reserved
  </tbody>
</table>

<h5 id=reserved-blocks>
Reserved block types</h5>

The <dfn>leading digit</dfn> of a block type is the first digit of its hexadecimal representation,
padded with zeros to at least 2 digits.
For example, the leading digits of 4<sub>16</sub>, 4B<sub>16</sub> and C10<sub>16</sub> are 0, 4 and C respectively.

A block type is <dfn>critical</dfn> if its [=leading digit=] is 0 through 9,
and <dfn>non-critical</dfn> if it is A through F.
A block whose type is not defined by this specification:

* Results in an [=unsupported failure=] ([[#interpretation]]) if its type is [=critical=];
* Is an <dfn>ignored block</dfn> otherwise.
    Like a mirror block, an ignored block doesn't belong to any [=segment=], MAY occur anywhere in [=archive/blocks=]
    and doesn't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=].

<div class=note>
  Every block type defined by this specification is [=critical=] except for [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]],
  which a decompressor not using them can skip.
  Future versions of this specification will use a critical type for a block
  without which an archive can't be correctly interpreted,
  e.g. a [[#data-block|data block]] with a new compression method in 38<sub>16</sub> through 3F<sub>16</sub>,
  and a non-critical type for a block that can be safely skipped, e.g. recovery data.
  The block-like structures below also have critical types, so they are never skipped by mistake.
</div>

Blocks whose type's [=leading digit=] is D
(i.e. D0<sub>16</sub> through DF<sub>16</sub>, D00<sub>16</sub> through DFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for development environments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to these blocks.
These blocks MUST be written or read only with specific instructions to enable them.

Blocks whose type's [=leading digit=] is E
(i.e. E0<sub>16</sub> through EF<sub>16</sub>, E00<sub>16</sub> through EFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for experiments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to those blocks.

<div class=note>
  Blocks reserved for development environments and experiments are [=non-critical=],
  so a decompressor not enabling them ignores them.
  An implementation enabling an experiment may treat its blocks as critical,
  since the experiment is expected to move to a block type defined by this specification once finished.
</div>

The following block types are defined as invalid due to the use as block-like structures.
A valid archive MUST NOT include such block types as a proper block.

* Block 278<sub>16</sub> ([=const/HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the unencrypted archive header.
* Block 69<sub>16</sub> ([=const/OVERLAY_HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the overlay archive header.
* Block 90&thinsp;5BCB&thinsp;957D<sub>16</sub> ([=const/FOOTER_TYPE=]) is used for the archive footer.
* Block 7FC<sub>16</sub> ([=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_TYPE=]) is used for the overlay archive footer.
* Block 155&thinsp;04DC&thinsp;A579<sub>16</sub> ([=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the encrypted archive header.

<h3 id=shared-external-structure>
Shared external structure</h3>

Many block types share the same external structure, only varying its internal structure.
The structure may or may not be compressed,
and uses a <dfn for=block>compression_method</dfn> and a <dfn for=block>checksum_method</dfn>
specified by the parent structure.
Each compression method defines the structure of its parameters ([=cdata/compression_params=]),
whose byte length is determined by parsing it.
Each [=checksum_method=] also has an associated, fixed <dfn for=block>checksum_size</dfn>.
<dfn for="">Checksummed Zstandard data</dfn> is the compressed external structure ([=block/compressed_data=])
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=]
and [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] as [=checksum_method=].

The compressed external structure (<dfn for=block>compressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=cdata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size_delta</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size_shrink</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=cdata/original_size_delta=] = 0
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compression_params</dfn>
      <td>Parameters of [=compression_method=]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=compression_method=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None, [=depends on=] [=checksum_method=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The compressed size (<dfn>compressed_size</dfn>) is inferred from the block size:
[=cdata/compressed_size=] = [=cdata/data_end=] - [=cdata/data_start=].
The original size (<dfn>original_size</dfn>) is encoded relative to [=cdata/compressed_size=]:

* If [=cdata/original_size_delta=] is not 0,
    [=cdata/original_size=] = [=cdata/compressed_size=] + [=cdata/original_size_delta=].
* Otherwise, [=cdata/original_size=] = [=cdata/compressed_size=] - [=cdata/original_size_shrink=],
    and [=cdata/original_size_shrink=] MUST be at most [=cdata/compressed_size=].

Each original size thus has exactly one encoding,
where the original size equal to [=cdata/compressed_size=] is encoded as two zero bytes.

<div class=note>
  Compressed data is usually shorter than the original, and costs a single field in that case.
  The original size can still be smaller, e.g. when Zstandard stores incompressible data in raw blocks,
  each of which has 3 bytes of overhead.
  This matters for blocks that always use Zstandard, such as [[#import-block|import blocks]]
  whose [=import/import_checksum=] doesn't compress,
  while a block that can use None as its [=compression_method=] typically does so instead.
</div>

</div>

The uncompressed external structure (<dfn for=block>uncompressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=udata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

In both structures, the checksum is computed over the [=decoded data=] ([[#block-contents]]),
i.e. the data after decompression, rather than the stored bytes ([[#checksums]]).


<h4 id=zstandard-compression-method>
Zstandard compression method</h4>

This specification only defines a single compression method, Zstandard [[!RFC8878]].
A <dfn export for="">Zstandard block</dfn> is the "Block" structure in the Zstandard frame ([[!RFC8878]], Section 3.1.1.2).
Its parameters ([=cdata/compression_params=]) are as follows:

<div dfn-for=zstd>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>window_descriptor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The meaning of the [=window_descriptor=] field is identical to the Zstandard's `Window_Descriptor` byte,
which determines `Window_Size` as in [[!RFC8878]], Section 3.1.1.1.2.
Consequently each Zstandard block holds at most `Block_Maximum_Size` = min(`Window_Size`, 128 KiB) bytes of data.

Conforming decompressors MUST support any `Window_Size` up to 2<sup>27</sup> bytes (128 MiB),
and MAY return a [=resource failure=] for a larger `Window_Size`.
Conforming compressors SHOULD NOT use a `Window_Size` larger than 2<sup>27</sup> bytes
unless explicitly requested.

A <dfn export for="">dictionary</dfn> is a byte sequence supplied to the compression method as if it preceded the data,
typically shared by multiple small blocks to compensate for the lack of shared compression context.
If a dictionary is in use, its content, entropy tables and repeat offsets are given by the dictionary block
as described in [[#dictionary-block]].

</div>

Data compressed by this method is a sequence of Zstandard blocks,
without an additional framing provided by Zstandard frames.
[=cdata/data=] MUST consist of one or more such blocks without any other bytes,
where only the last block is signalled as the final block by its block header.

<div class=note>
  The end of the compressed data is thus given both by [=cdata/data_end=] and by the final block,
  and they have to agree.
</div>

<h4 id=xxh64-checksum-method>
XXH64 checksum method</h4>

This specification only defines a single checksum method, XXH64 [[!XXHASH]].
Its seed MUST be zero and the result MUST be stored as [=u64le=].
The resulting [=checksum_size=] is 8.

<h3 id=spacer-block>
Spacer block</h3>

Blocks 0<sub>16</sub> through 3<sub>16</sub> are **spacer blocks**.
Their data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=spacer>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>space</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]]
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

A spacer block occupies any even number of bytes from 2,
which is sufficient since every block occupies an even number of bytes due to [=block/padding=].

Conforming compressors MUST fill [=spacer/space=] with zero bytes in order to avoid data leak,
but its content doesn't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=].
Conforming compressors are RECOMMENDED to utilize sparse files for performance.

<h3 id=size-hint-block>
Size hint block</h3>

Block 4<sub>16</sub> is a **size hint block**.
Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=szhint>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ 0
  </tbody>
</table>

If this block is present, it MUST be the first block after the header
and its [=szhint/size_hint=] field MUST be equal to the footer's [=archive/size_hint=] field.

</div>

<h3 id=unencrypted-archive>
Unencrypted archive</h3>

An **unencrypted archive** consists of a header, zero or more blocks and a footer.

<div dfn-for=archive>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] ∈ {[=const/HEADER_MAGIC=], [=const/OVERLAY_HEADER_MAGIC=]}
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint_block</dfn>
      <td>[[#size-hint-block|size_hint_block]]
      <td>[=cond/Optional=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>blocks</dfn>
      <td>[[#block|block]][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] ∈ {[=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=], [=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_MAGIC=]}
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_end=] - [=archive/footer_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=archive/archive_end=] - [=archive/archive_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>metadata_backward_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size_copy</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_size=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=archive/header_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to identify the file as an unencrypted Aqueous archive.
This header also marks the beginning of the archive, when the archive doesn't start at the beginning of the file.

[=archive/footer_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to mark the beginning of the footer.
The footer also marks the end of the archive, when the archive doesn't end at the end of the file.

An unencrypted archive is an <dfn export for="">overlay archive</dfn> if [=archive/header_magic=] is [=const/OVERLAY_HEADER_MAGIC=],
and a <dfn>standalone archive</dfn> otherwise.
[=archive/footer_magic=] MUST be [=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_MAGIC=] in an overlay archive and [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=] otherwise.
An overlay archive is interpreted against a <dfn export for="">base</dfn>,
an existing directory tree or file given separately from the overlay archive:
it starts from the base instead of an empty directory and applies its segments to it ([[#interpretation]]),
and only an overlay archive can contain [=external import blocks=] reading the base ([[#import-block]]).
The format of overlay archives is called the <dfn export>Aqueous overlay archive format</dfn>,
and has its own media type ([[#overlay-media-type]]).

<div class=note>
  A standalone archive is interpreted as an overlay archive would be with an empty directory as its base.
  Confusing the other way is harmful:
  an overlay archive that only adds and removes files, without any external import,
  would be silently interpreted without any file of its base.
  This can happen when an overlay archive with a damaged header is repaired into a standalone archive.
  Therefore every structure that can be found without the header tells whether the archive is an overlay archive:
  the footer, found from the end of the archive, and [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]], found by scanning.
  Metadata blocks are only found through them, and an encrypted archive tells it in its decrypted payload.
</div>

The offset between [=archive/header_magic=] and [=archive/footer_magic=] is recorded as [=archive/size_hint=],
which MAY also be replicated as [=archive/size_hint_block=] if random access is desired.

The footer size, which is the size of all fields between [=archive/footer_size=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=] (both exclusive),
is recorded both in [=archive/footer_size=] and in [=archive/footer_size_copy=],
so that the footer can be read from the end of the archive ([[#locating-an-archive]]).

<div class=note>
  [=archive/footer_size=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=] are [=u16le=] instead of [=leb128=].
  Therefore the footer size is limited to 65,535 bytes,
  and a single read of 65,545 bytes (including [=archive/footer_magic=], [=archive/footer_size=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=]) is
  sufficient to guarantee that the entire footer is read.
</div>

[=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] is the offset from [=archive/footer_start=] back to the beginning of the last metadata block,
just like [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the offset from the beginning of the current metadata block back to the previous one.
It MUST be 0 if and only if there is no metadata block,
so that a decompressor can tell such archives without scanning [=archive/blocks=].

[=archive/blocks=] are logically divided into [=segments=],
each consisting of [=content blocks=] and a single [[#metadata-block|metadata block]] describing them,
with optional spacer, [[#mirror-block|mirror]] and [=ignored blocks=] in between.
Each segment can be independently processed and decompressed in the reading order,
except that [=internal imports=] ([[#import-block]]) read the result of preceding segments.
Segments are described in [[#segments]].

Alternatively, [=archive/blocks=] can have no metadata block.
Such an archive decompresses into a single unnamed file,
whose content is the concatenation of the contents of all [=leaf blocks=] in the order of appearance.
It decompresses into an empty file if there is no leaf block.

<div class=note>
  This allows a single file of unknown size to be compressed in a streaming fashion,
  by writing each unit of random access as a separate leaf block, and thus a separate [=parcel=], as soon as it is compressed.
  It is analogous to a gzip file that doesn't need to contain the name of the decompressed file.
  Compressing multiple files in a streaming fashion is not supported:
  a file spanning multiple non-leaf blocks requires a [=parcel=] starting with a formula block,
  which declares the length of every slice of the non-leaf blocks before any of them.
</div>

</div>

<h3 id=locating-an-archive>
Locating an archive</h3>

A file may contain an archive with leading bytes, e.g. a self-extracting executable,
or with trailing bytes, e.g. appended by a transfer.
Since [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=] takes the archive alone,
a decompressor accepting such a file has to locate the archive first.

A header magic and a footer magic <dfn>match</dfn> when they are
[=const/HEADER_MAGIC=] and [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=], or [=const/OVERLAY_HEADER_MAGIC=] and [=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_MAGIC=].

To <dfn>locate an archive</dfn> in a [=byte sequence=] |file|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If |file| starts with [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=] or [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRRORED_HEADER_MAGIC=], return |file|.
    2. Let |back| be null.
        Let |n| be the last 2 bytes of |file| as [=u16le=], and let |f| be the length of |file| − |n| − 10.
        If |f| ≥ 0, the bytes of |file| from |f| are [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=] or [=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_MAGIC=] followed by |n| as [=u16le=],
        and the [=leb128=] from |f| + 8 is |s| such that |h| = |f| − |s| − 6 ≥ 0
        and the bytes of |file| from |h| are the header magic [=match|matching=] that footer magic, set |back| to |h|.
    3. If |file| starts with [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=] or [=const/OVERLAY_HEADER_MAGIC=]:
        1. If |back| is 0, return |file|.
        2. Read blocks from the offset 6 by the generic structure ([[#block]]),
            with [=block/data=] of every block taken as a [=byte sequence=] without parsing,
            until the bytes at the current offset |e| start with [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=] or [=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_MAGIC=].
            If it fails to read a block before that, or the footer magic doesn't [=match=] the header magic, return failure.
        3. Let |m| be the 2 bytes of |file| from |e| + 6 as [=u16le=].
            If |file| is shorter than |e| + |m| + 10 bytes, return failure.
            Otherwise, return the first |e| + |m| + 10 bytes of |file|.
    4. If |back| is not null, return the bytes of |file| from |back|.
    5. Return failure.
</ol>

The located archive is then [=interpret an archive|interpreted=], which may still fail.
A decompressor SHOULD warn the user when |file| has bytes outside the located archive.
It MAY search for [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=] and [=const/OVERLAY_HEADER_MAGIC=] when the archive can't be located otherwise,
e.g. when |file| has both leading and trailing bytes.

<div class=note>
  An encrypted archive is located only at the beginning of a file and extends to its end,
  because the end of its payload can't be known without decrypting it ([[#encrypted-archive]]).

  A block can't start with [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=] or [=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_MAGIC=],
  since they start with the shortest [=leb128=] encodings of [=const/FOOTER_TYPE=] and [=const/OVERLAY_FOOTER_TYPE=] ([[#constants]]),
  which are not valid block types.
  The step 3.2 therefore finds the end of the first archive in |file|,
  while the step 4 finds the archive at the end of |file| without reading the bytes before it.
  Archives concatenated together are not a single archive, and only the first one is located.
</div>

<h2 id=segments>
Segments and entries</h2>

<h3 id=archive-model>
Archive model</h3>

An <dfn>archive value</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="archive value">root</dfn>, either a [=directory node=] or a [=file node=].
    The latter represents a single unnamed file.
* <dfn for="archive value">extra</dfn>, an [=ordered map=] from integers to CBOR data items [[!RFC8949]]
    representing the archive-wide part of the [[#optional-metadata|optional metadata]].

A <dfn>file node</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="file node">content</dfn>, a [=byte sequence=].
* <dfn for="file node">link group</dfn>, a [=/link group=] or null.
* <dfn for="file node">extra</dfn>, a CBOR data item matching `aqr-entry-extra`, or null.

A <dfn>directory node</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="directory node">children</dfn>, an [=ordered map=] from [=entry names=]
    to [=file nodes=], [=directory nodes=], [=link nodes=], [=special nodes=] or [=unknown nodes=].
* <dfn for="directory node">extra</dfn>, a CBOR data item matching `aqr-entry-extra`, or null.

A <dfn>link node</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=], representing a symbolic link:

* <dfn for="link node">target</dfn>, a [=link target=].
* <dfn for="link node">extra</dfn>, a CBOR data item matching `aqr-entry-extra`, or null.

A <dfn>special node</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=], representing a FIFO or a device file:

* <dfn for="special node">kind</dfn>, one of "`fifo`", "`char-device`" or "`block-device`".
* <dfn for="special node">device</dfn>, a pair of non-negative integers (major, minor)
    if [=special node/kind=] is "`char-device`" or "`block-device`", or null otherwise.
* <dfn noexport for="special node">extra</dfn>, a CBOR data item matching `aqr-entry-extra`, or null.

<div class=note>
  A device number is stored as a pair, not as a platform-specific `dev_t` encoding,
  as tar and cpio do and as `makedev` takes it.
  Its meaning is up to the platform extracting it:
  the same pair generally denotes unrelated devices on different platforms,
  e.g. `/dev/null` is (1, 3) on Linux but (3, 2) on macOS,
  and this specification doesn't record which platform the number came from.
</div>

An <dfn>unknown node</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=],
representing a kind of node not defined by this version of this specification:

* <dfn for="unknown node">kind</dfn>, an integer greater than 4.
* <dfn for="unknown node">data</dfn>, a [=byte sequence=].
* <dfn noexport for="unknown node">extra</dfn>, a CBOR data item matching `aqr-entry-extra`, or null.

The [=archive value/root=] can't be a [=link node=], a [=special node=] or an [=unknown node=].

A <dfn>tombstone</dfn> is a [=struct=] with no [=struct/items=], marking the removal of a node.
It only occurs in [=directory node/children=] of the result of [=interpret a segment|interpreting a segment=],
and is removed by [=merge directory nodes|merging=] it into the preceding result ([[#segment-merging]]),
so it never occurs in an [=archive value=].

An <dfn>entry name</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="entry name">encoding</dfn>, either "`wtf-8`" or "`raw`".
* <dfn for="entry name">bytes</dfn>, a non-empty [=byte sequence=]
    which is a valid WTF-8 [[!WTF-8]] string if [=entry name/encoding=] is "`wtf-8`".

Two [=entry names=] are equal if and only if both items are equal.
In particular, a "`wtf-8`" name and a "`raw`" name are never equal even when their [=entry name/bytes=] are.

No [=entry name/bytes=] of an [=entry name=] can contain 00<sub>16</sub> or 2F<sub>16</sub> (`/`),
or be equal to (2E)<sub>16</sub> (`.`) or (2E 2E)<sub>16</sub> (`..`).

<div class=note>
  These restrictions make every path in an [=archive value=] a plain sequence of components,
  so that no name can refer to the current, parent or nested directory on any common file system.
  Platform-specific restrictions are handled at the extraction time ([[#extraction]]).
</div>

An [=entry name=] is encoded as a byte sequence in one of the following ways,
neither of which contains a zero byte:

* If its [=entry name/encoding=] is "`wtf-8`", its [=entry name/bytes=] as is.
    WTF-8 [[!WTF-8]] is an extension to UTF-8 and losslessly encodes a lone surrogate in UTF-16.
* If its [=entry name/encoding=] is "`raw`", byte C1<sub>16</sub> followed by its [=entry name/bytes=].

<div class=note>
  Byte C1<sub>16</sub> was chosen because it can't occur in WTF-8 or any other known extension to UTF-8.
</div>

Conforming compressors are RECOMMENDED to use the "`wtf-8`" encoding,
and SHOULD only use the "`raw`" encoding as a round-trip measure.

To <dfn>decode an entry name</dfn> from a [=meta/entry_name=] field |bytes|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If |bytes| is not empty and |bytes|[0] is C1<sub>16</sub>, let |name| be an [=entry name=]
        whose [=entry name/encoding=] is "`raw`" and [=entry name/bytes=] are |bytes| without the first byte.
    2. Otherwise, if |bytes| is a valid WTF-8 string, let |name| be an [=entry name=]
        whose [=entry name/encoding=] is "`wtf-8`" and [=entry name/bytes=] are |bytes|.
    3. Otherwise, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    4. If |name| violates any restriction of an [=entry name=] above, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    5. Return |name|.
</ol>

A <dfn>link target</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="link target">encoding</dfn>, either "`wtf-8`" or "`raw`".
* <dfn noexport for="link target">bytes</dfn>, a non-empty [=byte sequence=] not containing 00<sub>16</sub>,
    which is a valid WTF-8 [[!WTF-8]] string if [=link target/encoding=] is "`wtf-8`".

Unlike an [=entry name=], a [=link target=] is a path which may contain 2F<sub>16</sub> (`/`) as a separator,
and components equal to `.` or `..`.
It may be absolute, and may refer to a path that doesn't exist in the [=archive value=] or anywhere else.
The separator is always `/`, even for a link target from a platform using `\` as the separator, such as Windows,
where every `\` is represented as `/`.
Otherwise a link target is not normalized, and its meaning, e.g. of a drive letter, is left to the platform.
There is no limit on its length other than implementation-defined limits.

<div class=note>
  `/` can't occur in a file name on Windows, and isn't interpreted as a separator in a link target there,
  so this replacement is lossless.
  A decompressor on Windows replaces `/` back with `\` when creating a link ([[#extraction]]).

  On Windows, the target of an absolute link or a junction is stored as an NT path starting with `\??\`,
  which has no meaning on other platforms.
  A compressor is advised to convert it to a Win32 path first,
  e.g. `\??\C:\foo` to `C:/foo`, `\??\UNC\server\share\x` to `//server/share/x`
  and `\??\Volume{GUID}\x` to `//?/Volume{GUID}/x`.

  Platforms differ in the maximum length of a link target:
  4095 bytes on Linux (less on some file systems, e.g. 1023 bytes on XFS),
  1023 bytes on macOS and FreeBSD, and about 8,000 UTF-16 code units on Windows.
  A link that can't be created is handled at the extraction time ([[#extraction]]).
</div>

To <dfn>decode a link target</dfn> from a [=meta/link_target=] field |bytes|,
do the same as to [=decode an entry name=] from |bytes|, except that
the result is a [=link target=] and restrictions of a [=link target=] apply instead.

A [=link target=] is never resolved within the archive model.
Every path in an [=archive value=] is still a plain sequence of components,
and a lookup of a path in an [=archive value=] never follows a [=link node=], as `lstat` doesn't.
In particular, no node is a descendant of a [=link node=].

<div class=note>
  This makes the classic attack of extracting `a/passwd` through a preceding link `a` pointing to `/etc`
  inexpressible in the archive model.
  A tool MAY offer a lookup following links, but it then has to limit the number of links followed.
</div>

A <dfn>link group</dfn> is an opaque value that is only compared for equality,
and is either a pair of a segment and a [=segment-local=] integer or an [=archive-wide=] integer ([[#link-groups]]).
Two [=archive values=] are equal if and only if they become identical by some one-to-one renaming of the [=/link groups=] of one of them.

<h3 id=content-blocks>
Content blocks</h3>

Any block other than spacer, size hint, metadata, [[#mirror-block|mirror]] and [=ignored blocks=]
is a <dfn>content block</dfn> which has to be described by a subsequent [[#metadata-block|metadata block]].
A metadata block and all content blocks between the previous metadata block (or the beginning of the archive if none)
and that metadata block form a <dfn>segment</dfn>.
As an exception, an archive without a metadata block ([[#unencrypted-archive]]) forms a single segment with no metadata block.
Each segment MUST be self-contained:
every reference from a block in a segment, whether by an index or by an offset, MUST point to a block in the same segment.
An [=internal import=] ([[#import-block]]) refers to a node of preceding segments by its path rather than to a block,
and is the only way for a segment to use the content of another segment.

Content blocks are classified as follows:

* A <dfn>leaf block</dfn> represents the content of one or more files by itself.
    Leaf [[#data-block|data blocks]] and [[#formula-block|formula blocks]] are leaf blocks.
* A <dfn>non-leaf block</dfn> doesn't represent any file by itself,
    and only supplies data referenced by one or more formula blocks.
    Non-leaf [[#data-block|data blocks]] and [[#import-block|import blocks]] are non-leaf blocks.
    Each non-leaf block supplies one or more <dfn>non-leaf items</dfn> to formula blocks:
    a non-leaf data block supplies a single item, and an import block supplies one item for each of its [=import/import|imports=].
* An <dfn>auxiliary block</dfn> supplies data used to decode other blocks.
    [[#dictionary-block|Dictionary blocks]], [[#module-block|module blocks]] and [[#resource-block|resource blocks]]
    are auxiliary blocks.

Dictionary, module and resource blocks are respectively indexed in the order of appearance within the segment.
Two conventions are used for these indices:

* Fields in content blocks use <em>0-based</em> indices,
    because the presence of a reference is signalled separately (e.g. by the block type).
* Fields in the metadata block that may indicate the absence of a reference use <em>1-based</em> indices,
    where zero denotes the absence.

<h4 id=block-order>
Block order</h4>

Blocks in a segment MUST be ordered so that a formula block is resolved by blocks preceding it
and all data required to evaluate it follows it:

* A dictionary block MUST precede any block using it.
* A module or resource block MUST precede any formula block referencing it.
* Every [=non-leaf item=] MUST be referenced by at least one formula block,
    and every non-leaf block MUST follow all formula blocks referencing its items.
* Leaf blocks are otherwise not constrained and MAY be interleaved with non-leaf blocks.

<div class=note>
  These rules make every reference from a formula block to a non-leaf block a forward reference.
  Therefore, when a non-leaf block is read in a streaming fashion,
  all formula blocks referencing it have already been read,
  and the decompressor knows exactly when the block can be discarded.
  Likewise, the decompressor never has to retain a formula block after all non-leaf blocks it references have been read.
  An import block whose imports are referenced by many formula blocks follows all of them,
  so a streaming decompressor retains those formula blocks until the import block is read.
  A compressor bounds this by spreading imports over multiple import blocks.
</div>

A leaf block and all subsequent content blocks up to the next leaf block or the end of the segment
form a <dfn>parcel</dfn>.
When no leaf block is interleaved with non-leaf blocks,
a parcel consists of a leaf block, the non-leaf blocks it references if it is a formula block,
and possibly [=auxiliary blocks=].
In that case a decompressor can read a single file by reading its parcel and the [=auxiliary blocks=] it uses,
and a streaming decompressor never has to retain a formula block beyond its parcel.

<figure id=fig-segments>
  <pre class=include-raw>path: figures/segments.svg</pre>
  <figcaption>
    An archive with two segments, each ending with the metadata block describing it,
    and four [=parcels=], each starting with a leaf block.
    Arrows denote references from formula blocks to non-leaf blocks (solid) and to a module block (dashed).
    The module block precedes the first leaf block in its segment, so it belongs to no parcel.
    Dictionary, resource and spacer blocks are omitted,
    and no leaf block is interleaved with non-leaf blocks.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h4 id=registered-blocks>
Registered dictionaries and modules</h4>

A <dfn>registered dictionary</dfn> is an entry of the Aqueous Dictionaries Registry ([[#dictionaries-registry]]),
and a <dfn>registered module</dfn> is an entry of the Aqueous Modules Registry ([[#modules-registry]]).
Each entry is identified by its <dfn>registered hash</dfn>,
the SHA-512/256 hash [[!FIPS180-4]] of its bytes:
the [=decoded data=] of a dictionary block for a registered dictionary ([[#dictionary-block]]),
and [=module/wasm_module=] for a registered module ([[#module-block]]).

Block 50<sub>16</sub> (a dictionary block) and block 60<sub>16</sub> (a module block) are <dfn>shorthand blocks</dfn>.
The [=block/data=] of a shorthand block is a [=registered hash=] alone, 32 bytes long,
instead of [=checksummed Zstandard data=],
and the shorthand block stands for a block of the same kind whose decoded data is the bytes of that entry:

* [=block/size=] MUST be 32.
* The [=registered hash=] of block 50<sub>16</sub> MUST be that of a [=registered dictionary=],
    which also gives [=dict/has_entropy_tables=],
    and that of block 60<sub>16</sub> MUST be that of a [=registered module=].
* If the decompressor doesn't know the entry of the [=registered hash=] in the corresponding registry,
    an [=unsupported failure=] occurs ([[#interpretation]]).
* A shorthand block is otherwise the same as the dictionary or module block it stands for,
    e.g. in indexing, [[#block-order|block order]] and [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]].

The two registries share the hash function but not the entries,
and a shorthand block is resolved only against the registry of its own kind.
Knowing an entry of one registry never makes a decompressor know an entry of the other:
in particular, the hash of a registered module in block 50<sub>16</sub> doesn't denote a dictionary of the module's bytes,
unless the same bytes are separately registered as a dictionary, in which case they are two unrelated entries.

A shorthand block has no checksum, and the [=registered hash=] is only looked up and never verified.
Therefore damage to the hash can't be distinguished from a hash that was never registered,
and both result in an [=unsupported failure=] rather than an [=invalid archive=] failure.

<div class=note>
  Registry entries are never modified or removed, so a [=registered hash=] keeps denoting the same bytes,
  and a decompressor may know only some of the entries.
  Keeping the registries apart lets a decompressor carry a native implementation of a registered module
  ([[#module-block]]) instead of its bytes, which it couldn't if the hash could also denote a dictionary.
  A damaged hash is found as unknown with an overwhelming probability,
  and a [[#mirror-block|mirror block]] of the shorthand block can recover it.
</div>

<h3 id=metadata-block>
Metadata block</h3>

Blocks 10<sub>16</sub> through 17<sub>16</sub> are **metadata blocks**,
whose [=block/type=] is <code>0001&thinsp;0|m||ss|</code> in binary ([[#block-types]]).
Their data uses the shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] as [=checksum_method=]
and [=compression_method=] given by |m|: None if |m| is 0, and [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] if |m| is 1.

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<div dfn-for=meta>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>previous_metadata_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_modules</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_resources</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_dictionaries</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_directories</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_files</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_specials</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=meta/entries_end=] - [=meta/entries_start=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>module_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_modules=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>resource_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_resources=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_dictionaries=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_parents</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_directories=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] - 1]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_params</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/file_param=][[=meta/num_files=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/entries_size=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] - 1]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/entry=][[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=meta/optional_metadata_size=]]
  </tbody>
</table>

All relative offsets are measured backward from the beginning of the current metadata block:
a relative offset |o| refers to the block starting |o| bytes before the current metadata block,
implying that the offset of zero is invalid.
All valid relative offsets are also even.

Each metadata block describes the [=content blocks=] in its [=segment=],
i.e. blocks appearing after the previous metadata block, if any, and until the current metadata block.
The previous metadata block is linked via [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=],
which is also the maximum possible offset that can occur in this particular metadata block.
If there is no prior metadata block,
[=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the relative offset
to the end of [=archive/header_magic=] in the unencrypted archive structure ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
plus 1 so that it never points to a valid block.

[=meta/num_modules=], [=meta/num_resources=] and [=meta/num_dictionaries=] are
the numbers of module, resource and dictionary blocks in the [=segment=] respectively.
Relative offsets to those blocks are
later recorded as [=meta/module_offsets=], [=meta/resource_offsets=] and [=meta/dictionary_offsets=] respectively,
in the order of appearance.

The metadata block represents zero or more directories, zero or more files and zero or more <dfn>special entries</dfn>,
collectively called [=meta/entries=] and numbered together.
The root directory is numbered 0,
subsequent directories are numbered from 1 to [=meta/num_directories=],
individual files are numbered from ([=meta/num_directories=] + 1) to ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=]),
and [=special entries=] are numbered from ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + 1)
to ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=]).
There MUST be at least one entry other than the root directory,
i.e. [=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] ≥ 1.
[=meta/entry_parents=][|k| - 2] is the parent of the entry |k| for each |k| ≥ 2,
and MUST be a directory less than |k|,
i.e. in [0, min(|k| - 1, [=meta/num_directories=])], so that directories can't form a cycle.
The root directory (entry 0) has no parent and the parent of the entry 1 is always the root directory,
so they are omitted.

Issue: Whether a segment without any entry other than the root directory should be allowed,
e.g. to update only the archive-wide optional metadata or the [=entry extra=] of the root directory.

File parameters (<dfn>file_param</dfn>) occur for each file (but not for directories or special entries), and consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_dictionary</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_dictionaries=])
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_output</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=meta/file_offset=] is odd
  </tbody>
</table>

[=meta/file_dictionary=] is the dictionary used by the block that [=meta/file_offset=] refers to
as a <em>1-based</em> index, or zero if no dictionary is used.
Therefore [=meta/file_dictionary=] = |k| refers to the block at [=meta/dictionary_offsets=][|k| - 1],
and [=meta/num_dictionaries=] is the maximum possible value for it.
A relative offset to the corresponding [=leaf block=], which starts the file's [=parcel=],
is recorded as [=meta/file_offset=], plus 1 if the file is another output of the block than the output 0.
The relative offset can't be zero.
Since every relative offset is even, [=meta/file_offset=] % 2 tells which:
the <dfn>file output index</dfn> is 0 if [=meta/file_offset=] is even, and [=meta/file_output=] + 1 if it is odd.
Multiple files MAY refer to the same leaf block and output, in which case they have the same content.

<div class=note>
  This is how an archive stores files with the same content only once,
  including files that were hard links to each other ([[#link-groups]]).
  It costs only a [=meta/file_param=] and an [=meta/entry=] per file.
</div>

Entry structure (<dfn>entry</dfn>) then occurs for each directory, file and special entry, in this order, and consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_name</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*] ≠ 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>entry_name_sentinel</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a file
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>special_kind</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>link_target</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*] ≠ 0
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] = 1
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>link_target_sentinel</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] = 1
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>device_major</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] is 3 or 4
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>device_minor</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] is 3 or 4
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>special_data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] > 4
  </tbody>
</table>

Each entry is named according to [=meta/entry_name=],
which encodes an [=entry name=] as described in [[#archive-model]] and is [=decode an entry name|decoded=] there.
Entry names from ZIP are discussed in [[#entry-names-from-zip]].

A special entry represents a node other than a file or a directory, as determined by [=meta/special_kind=]:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>[=meta/special_kind=]
      <th>Node
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>0
      <td>[=Tombstone=], which removes the node at the same path in preceding segments or in the [=base=] ([[#segment-merging]])
    <tr>
      <td>1
      <td>[=Link node=], with [=meta/link_target=] as its target
    <tr>
      <td>2
      <td>[=Special node=] of the kind "`fifo`"
    <tr>
      <td>3
      <td>[=Special node=] of the kind "`char-device`", with [=meta/device_major=] and [=meta/device_minor=] as its device number
    <tr>
      <td>4
      <td>[=Special node=] of the kind "`block-device`", with [=meta/device_major=] and [=meta/device_minor=] as its device number
    <tr>
      <td>5 or greater
      <td>[=Unknown node=], reserved for future versions of this specification
  </tbody>
</table>

[=meta/link_target=] uses the same encodings as [=meta/entry_name=].
[=meta/special_data=] is not interpreted by this version of this specification;
its length is determined by the beginning of the next entry in [=meta/entry_offsets=],
or [=meta/entries_end=] for the last entry.

<div class=note>
  Special entries are numbered separately from files, as directories are,
  so that [=meta/file_params=] remain directly indexed by entry numbers.
  A link target is stored in the entry rather than in the [[#optional-metadata|optional metadata]],
  because it defines the node itself and can't be ignored.
  Since the metadata block is compressed as a whole, similar targets compress well.

  Unknown kinds are not an [=invalid archive=] failure,
  so that a decompressor can still extract the rest of an archive using a kind defined in the future.
  Every special entry can be skipped without knowing its kind, thanks to [=meta/entry_offsets=].
  An unknown node replaces an earlier node at the same path like any other non-directory node ([[#segment-merging]]),
  so a future kind can't change how segments are merged.
  This is why tombstones are defined by this version, as the first kind:
  a decompressor treating a tombstone as an unknown node would keep it in place of the node it was meant to remove.
</div>

The [=meta/entries=] array is the only part that has the unspecified number of bytes,
which is therefore available as [=meta/entries_size=] beforehand.
Any other field can be randomly accessed after reading the initial set of [=leb128=] fields.

As an exception to the relative offsets above, [=meta/entry_offsets=] are measured from [=meta/entries_start=].
The entry 1 always starts at [=meta/entries_start=], so its offset is omitted,
and [=meta/entry_offsets=][|k| - 2] is the offset to the beginning of the entry |k| for each |k| ≥ 2.
They MUST agree with the actual positions of [=meta/entries=].

[=meta/optional_metadata=] is described in Optional metadata ([[#optional-metadata]]).

</div>

<h3 id=interpretation>
Interpreting an archive</h3>

To <dfn export>interpret an archive</dfn> given a [=byte sequence=] |input|, an optional passphrase |passphrase|
and an optional [=base=] |base|, which is an [=archive value=]:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If |input| starts with [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=] or [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRRORED_HEADER_MAGIC=]:
        1. Parse |input| as the encrypted archive structure ([[#encrypted-archive]]) in its entirety.
            If it fails to parse, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
        2. If [=envelope/header_checksum=] is present and doesn't match, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
        3. If [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] exceeds an implementation-defined limit, return a [=resource failure=].
        4. If |passphrase| is not given, or the file key can't be unwrapped from [=envelope/encrypted_file_key=]
            with |passphrase| as described in [[!AGE]], return a [=decryption failure=].
        5. Using the unwrapped file key, verify [=envelope/age_header_mac=] against the reconstructed age header
            and decrypt the [=age payload=] as described in [[!AGE]].
            If either fails, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
        6. Set |input| to the decrypted payload.
            It MUST satisfy the additional constraints in [[#encrypted-archive]],
            or an [=invalid archive=] failure is returned.
    2. If |input| parses as the unencrypted archive structure ([[#unencrypted-archive]]) in its entirety,
        with [=block/data=] of every block taken as a [=byte sequence=] without parsing,
        and either the type of any of [=archive/blocks=] is [=critical=] and not defined by this specification,
        or any of [=archive/blocks=] is a [=shorthand block=] of 32 bytes of [=block/data=]
        whose [=registered hash=] the decompressor doesn't know ([[#registered-blocks]]),
        return an [=unsupported failure=].
    3. Parse |input| as the unencrypted archive structure ([[#unencrypted-archive]]) in its entirety.
        If it fails to parse, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    4. If |input| is an [=overlay archive=] and |base| is not given, return a [=base failure=].
    5. If |input| is a [=standalone archive=], set |base| to an [=archive value=]
        whose [=archive value/root=] is a [=directory node=] with an empty map as [=directory node/children=]
        and null as [=directory node/extra=], and whose [=archive value/extra=] is an empty map.
    6. If [=archive/blocks=] contain no [[#metadata-block|metadata block]]:
        1. Let |content| be the concatenation of the [=file contents=] of all [=leaf blocks=] in [=archive/blocks=],
            in the order of appearance, each for every output index from 0 in order
            (all outputs of a formula block, and the only one of a data block).
        2. Return an [=archive value=] whose [=archive value/root=] is a [=file node=]
            with |content| as [=file node/content=], null as [=file node/link group=] and null as [=file node/extra=],
            and whose [=archive value/extra=] is an empty map.
    7. If the [=archive value/root=] of |base| is a [=directory node=], let |root| be a copy of it.
        Otherwise let |root| be a [=directory node=] with an empty map as [=directory node/children=] and null as [=directory node/extra=].
    8. Let |extra| be a copy of the [=archive value/extra=] of |base|.
    9. For each [=segment=] |segment| in [=archive/blocks=], in the order of appearance:
        1. Let |segment root| be the result of [=interpret a segment|interpreting=] |segment|.
        2. [=Merge directory nodes|Merge=] |segment root| into |root|.
        3. For each key |k| and value |v| in the top-level map of the segment's [[#optional-metadata|optional metadata]]
            other than `entry-extras`, set |extra|[|k|] to |v|.
    10. Return an [=archive value=] with |root| as [=archive value/root=] and |extra| as [=archive value/extra=].
</ol>

The <dfn>archive base</dfn> is |base| after the step 5,
and the <dfn>preceding result</dfn> of a [=segment=] is |root| just before the segment is interpreted in the step 9,
or the [=archive value/root=] of the archive base for an archive without a metadata block.
They are what [=external imports=] and [=internal imports=] respectively refer to ([[#import-resolution]]).
A given |base| doesn't affect the interpretation of a [=standalone archive=].

<div class=note>
  The preceding result of the first segment of an [=overlay archive=] is its base,
  so an internal import can also read a file of the base, as long as no preceding segment has replaced or removed it.
  An external import reads the base as given, even after a preceding segment has replaced the file.
  An overlay archive without a metadata block replaces its base with a single file,
  e.g. to patch a single file given as the base.
</div>

The input to this algorithm is exactly the archive, from the first byte of [=archive/header_magic=]
to the last byte of [=archive/footer_size_copy=].

A file may contain an archive with leading or trailing bytes,
which a decompressor removes by [=locate an archive|locating the archive=] ([[#locating-an-archive]]).

Since every [=content block=] has to be described by a subsequent metadata block ([[#content-blocks]]),
an archive with a metadata block can't have any content block after the last metadata block.
[=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] and [=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] MUST point to
the metadata blocks found by parsing [=archive/blocks=] as described in [[#metadata-block]] and [[#unencrypted-archive]],
so that a decompressor can find each [=segment=] either by scanning blocks forward or by following offsets backward.

<h4 id=segment-interpretation>
Interpreting a segment</h4>

To <dfn>interpret a segment</dfn> |segment| whose metadata block is |metadata block|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. Let |meta| be the [=decoded data=] of |metadata block|, parsed as its internal structure ([[#metadata-block]]).
    2. Let |d| be [=meta/num_directories=], |f| be [=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=]
        and |n| be [=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] of |meta|.
    3. Let |nodes| be a [=list=] containing a single [=directory node=]
        with an empty map as [=directory node/children=] and the [=entry extra=] for the entry 0 as [=directory node/extra=].
    4. For each |k| in [1, |n|]:
        1. Let |entry| be |meta|.[=meta/entries=][|k| - 1].
        2. Let |name| be the result of [=decode an entry name|decoding=] |entry|.[=meta/entry_name=].
        3. Let |parent| be 0 if |k| = 1, or |meta|.[=meta/entry_parents=][|k| - 2] otherwise.
        4. If |k| ≤ |d|, let |node| be a [=directory node=] with an empty map as [=directory node/children=].
        5. Otherwise, if |k| ≤ |f|:
            1. Let |param| be |meta|.[=meta/file_params=][|k| - |d| - 1].
            2. Let |block| be the block at the relative offset |param|.[=meta/file_offset=] - |param|.[=meta/file_offset=] % 2
                from |metadata block|. It MUST be a [=leaf block=] in |segment|.
            3. If |param|.[=meta/file_dictionary=] is zero, |block| MUST NOT use a dictionary.
                Otherwise, |block| MUST use the dictionary block at |meta|.[=meta/dictionary_offsets=][|param|.[=meta/file_dictionary=] - 1].
            4. Let |content| be the [=file content=] of |block| for the [=file output index=] of |param|.
                Its length MUST be |entry|.[=meta/original_size=].
            5. Let |node| be a [=file node=] with |content| as [=file node/content=] and null as [=file node/link group=].
            6. If the [=entry extra=] for the entry |k| has `link-group` with a value |g|,
                set |node|.[=file node/link group=] to a pair of |segment| and |g| if |g| ≥ 0, or to |g| otherwise.
        6. Otherwise, if |entry|.[=meta/special_kind=] is 0, let |node| be a [=tombstone=].
            The [=entry extra=] for the entry |k| MUST be null.
        7. Otherwise, if |entry|.[=meta/special_kind=] is 1:
            1. Let |target| be the result of [=decode a link target|decoding=] |entry|.[=meta/link_target=].
            2. Let |node| be a [=link node=] with |target| as [=link node/target=].
        8. Otherwise, if |entry|.[=meta/special_kind=] is 2, let |node| be a [=special node=]
            with "`fifo`" as [=special node/kind=] and null as [=special node/device=].
        9. Otherwise, if |entry|.[=meta/special_kind=] is 3 or 4, let |node| be a [=special node=]
            with "`char-device`" or "`block-device`" respectively as [=special node/kind=]
            and (|entry|.[=meta/device_major=], |entry|.[=meta/device_minor=]) as [=special node/device=].
        10. Otherwise, let |node| be an [=unknown node=]
            with |entry|.[=meta/special_kind=] as [=unknown node/kind=] and |entry|.[=meta/special_data=] as [=unknown node/data=].
        11. If |node| is not a [=tombstone=], set the extra of |node| to the [=entry extra=] for the entry |k|.
        12. Let |children| be the [=directory node/children=] of |nodes|[|parent|].
            |children|[|name|] MUST NOT [=map/exist=].
        13. Set |children|[|name|] to |node|.
        14. [=list/Append=] |node| to |nodes|.
    5. Return |nodes|[0].
</ol>

Since every |parent| is less than |k| and at most |d| by [[#metadata-block]], |nodes|[|parent|] is always a [=directory node=] already in |nodes|.

The <dfn>entry extra</dfn> for the entry |k| is the element at the <em>0-based</em> index |k| of `entry-extras`
in the segment's optional metadata, where the index 0 corresponds to the root directory,
or null if `entry-extras` or such element doesn't exist.
`entry-extras` MUST NOT have more elements than the entries including the root directory,
i.e. [=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] + 1.

<h4 id=segment-merging>
Merging segments</h4>

To <dfn>merge directory nodes</dfn> |later| into |earlier|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. For each |name| → |node| of |later|.[=directory node/children=]:
        1. Let |existing| be |earlier|.[=directory node/children=][|name|], or null if it doesn't [=map/exist=].
        2. If |node| is a [=tombstone=], [=map/remove=] |earlier|.[=directory node/children=][|name|] if it [=map/exists=].
        3. Otherwise, if |node| is a [=directory node=]:
            1. If |existing| is not a [=directory node=], set |existing| to a [=directory node=]
                with an empty map as [=directory node/children=] and null as [=directory node/extra=],
                and set |earlier|.[=directory node/children=][|name|] to |existing|.
            2. [=Merge directory nodes|Merge=] |node| into |existing|.
        4. Otherwise, set |earlier|.[=directory node/children=][|name|] to |node|.
    2. If |later|.[=directory node/extra=] is not null, set |earlier|.[=directory node/extra=] to it.
</ol>

As a result, a directory appearing in multiple segments has the union of their children,
while any other node, or a directory replaced by a node of the different kind, is replaced as a whole with its descendants.
In particular, a directory replaced by a [=link node=] loses all its descendants,
so no node is a descendant of a [=link node=] in the result.
A [=tombstone=] removes the node at its path along with its descendants, and does nothing if there is no such node.
A tombstone is never kept in the result, even under a directory that didn't exist before.
A directory with a null [=directory node/extra=] in a later segment keeps the existing [=directory node/extra=],
which can instead be replaced with an empty map `{}`.

<div class=note>
  Directories necessarily reappear in later segments to hold new files under them,
  which is why a directory is merged with an existing one rather than replacing it.
  For the same reason, a reappearing directory doesn't have to repeat its [=entry extra=].
</div>

<div class=note>
  A directory whose children change entirely, e.g. from |A| to |B|, keeps its node and gets tombstones for the names in |A| but not in |B|,
  while the names in |B| replace or add nodes as usual.
  A tombstone on a directory states that the directory itself is absent from the result,
  and a single tombstone removes a whole subtree.
  So replacing the entire base of an [=overlay archive=] needs a tombstone for each name only in the base
  at the topmost level where the base and the new tree differ.
</div>

<h2 id=stored-data>
Stored data</h2>

<h3 id=data-block>
Data block</h3>

Blocks 20<sub>16</sub> through 37<sub>16</sub> and 40<sub>16</sub> through 4B<sub>16</sub> are **data blocks**.
A data block is a **compressed block** if its [=compression_method=] is not None,
and an **uncompressed block** otherwise.

<div dfn-for=dblock>

The [=block/type=] of a data block is <code>001|kk|&thinsp;|c||ss|</code> in binary ([[#block-types]]) for a [=leaf block=],
and <code>0100&thinsp;|kk||ss|</code> for a [=non-leaf block=], where:

* |kk| determines [=compression_method=] and whether a dictionary is used (<dfn>has_dictionary</dfn>):
    0 for None without dictionary, 1 for [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] without dictionary,
    and 2 for [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] with dictionary.
    3 is reserved for future compression methods.
* |c| determines [=checksum_method=]: None if 0, and [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] if 1.
    A non-leaf data block always uses [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] ([[#checksums]]).

Its data consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=dblock/has_dictionary=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compressed_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/compressed_data=]
      <td>[=If=] [=compression_method=] is not None
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>uncompressed_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/uncompressed_data=]
      <td>[=If=] [=compression_method=] is None
  </tbody>
</table>

If a dictionary is in use, its <em>0-based</em> index is specified as [=dblock/dictionary_index=].
For a leaf data block, the metadata block also gives the dictionary as [=meta/file_dictionary=],
which is available before reading the block;
for a non-leaf data block, [=dblock/dictionary_index=] is the only reference to the dictionary.
[=dblock/compressed_data=] and [=dblock/uncompressed_data=] use the shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]])
with [=compression_method=] and [=checksum_method=] determined as above.
[=dblock/uncompressed_data=] represents a raw, uninterpreted byte sequence.

A compressed block is a single unit of decompression:
reading any part of its content requires decompressing it from the beginning.
Content larger than a desired unit of random access is split into multiple blocks:

* In general, a file is represented by a [[#formula-block|formula block]] concatenating multiple non-leaf blocks,
    which typically form a single [=parcel=] together.
    The formula block then acts as an index for random access within the parcel.
* In an archive without a metadata block ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
    the file is simply represented by multiple leaf blocks.

</div>

<h3 id=dictionary-block>
Dictionary block</h3>

Blocks 50<sub>16</sub> through 57<sub>16</sub> are **dictionary blocks**,
whose [=block/type=] is <code>0101&thinsp;0|t||ss|</code> in binary ([[#block-types]]).
Their data is [=checksummed Zstandard data=] ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
except that block 50<sub>16</sub> is a [=shorthand block=] for a [=registered dictionary=] ([[#registered-blocks]]).

<div dfn-for=dict>

|t| determines whether the dictionary has entropy tables (<dfn>has_entropy_tables</dfn>): false if 0, and true if 1.

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entropy_tables</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>[=If=] [=dict/has_entropy_tables=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>repeat_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u32le=][3]
      <td>[=If=] [=dict/has_entropy_tables=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>content</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

The decoded data of a dictionary block is a [=dictionary=] for blocks using it,
and corresponds to a Zstandard dictionary ([[!RFC8878]], Section 5) without `Magic_Number` and `Dictionary_ID`:

*   If [=dict/has_entropy_tables=] is true, [=dict/entropy_tables=] is the `Entropy_Tables` structure,
    whose length is determined by parsing it, and [=dict/repeat_offsets=] are the three `Repeat_Offsets` in order.
    Each repeat offset MUST be in [1, length of [=dict/content=]].
*   Otherwise, it is a raw content dictionary:
    the entropy tables start empty and the repeat offsets start at their initial values (1, 4, 8),
    even when [=dict/content=] begins with the dictionary `Magic_Number`.

In both cases, [=dict/content=] is the `Content` of the dictionary,
which is the only part of the dictionary that sequences can reference as preceding data.
[=dict/content=] MUST be at least 8 bytes long.

<div class=note>
  `Magic_Number` and `Dictionary_ID` are omitted because they are only meaningful for Zstandard frames
  and the framing is done by this format.
  An implementation using libzstd can prepend them (with an arbitrary nonzero `Dictionary_ID`)
  to load a dictionary with entropy tables.
  In that case it has to make sure that no sequence references the prepended bytes or [=dict/entropy_tables=],
  which some decompression paths of libzstd don't check.
</div>

<div class=note>
  Entropy tables can be trained independently of the content (e.g. `ZDICT_finalizeDictionary`)
  and mainly benefit small blocks:
  in measurements with per-file blocks of 1–20 KB files, they reduced the size by 0.4–26%,
  while the benefit vanished for blocks of 64 KB or more.
  Fast strategies of libzstd (e.g. levels 1–3 of 1.5.7) reuse the dictionary's entropy tables for every block
  even when the block's own tables would be smaller.
  When the repeat offsets of the dictionary are the initial values,
  a compressor can avoid this by also compressing a block with the same [=dict/content=] as a raw content dictionary
  and choosing the smaller result,
  since a block compressed that way never refers to the entropy tables of the dictionary.
</div>

</div>

<h3 id=block-contents>
Block contents</h3>

The <dfn>decoded data</dfn> of a block using the shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
given its [=compression_method=] and [=checksum_method=], is determined as follows:

* If [=compression_method=] is None, it is [=udata/data=].
* If [=compression_method=] is [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]],
    it is the result of decompressing [=cdata/data=] as described there, with [=cdata/compression_params=]
    and the [=dictionary=] given by the dictionary block in use ([[#dictionary-block]]), if any.
    The result MUST have exactly [=cdata/original_size=] bytes.
* If [=checksum_method=] is not None, the checksum MUST be equal to the checksum of the decoded data
    computed by the [=checksum_method=].

The <dfn>stored content</dfn> of a [[#data-block|data block]],
whether a [=leaf block=] or not, is the [=decoded data=] of its [=dblock/compressed_data=] or [=dblock/uncompressed_data=].
The stored content of a [=non-leaf item=] is the stored content of the non-leaf data block supplying it,
or the [=imported content=] of the [=import/import=] supplying it ([[#import-resolution]]).
A data block using a dictionary uses the dictionary block at [=dblock/dictionary_index=] within the [=segment=].

The <dfn>file content</dfn> of a [=leaf block=] for an output index |i| is:

* Its [=stored content=], if it is a data block. |i| MUST be 0.
* The output at the index |i| of [=evaluate a formula block|evaluating=] it, if it is a [[#formula-block|formula block]].
    |i| MUST be less than its [=formula/num_outputs=].

<h2 id=formulas>
Formulas</h2>

<h3 id=formula-block>
Formula block</h3>

Blocks 70<sub>16</sub> through 77<sub>16</sub> are **formula blocks**,
whose [=block/type=] is <code>0111&thinsp;0|d||ss|</code> in binary ([[#block-types]]).

<div dfn-for=formula>

|d| determines whether a dictionary is used (<dfn>has_dictionary</dfn>): false if 0, and true if 1.

Its data consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=formula/has_dictionary=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_outputs</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>formula_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/compressed_data=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If a dictionary is in use, its <em>0-based</em> index is specified as [=formula/dictionary_index=], as in data blocks.

[=formula/num_outputs=] is the number of outputs of the formula block, which MUST be at least 1 ([[#formula-evaluation]]).

<div class=note>
  Every instruction pops and pushes a number of items that its operands give,
  so the number of outputs follows from the instructions alone, without applying any module.
  [=formula/num_outputs=] tells it before [=formula/formula_data=] is decompressed,
  e.g. to a decompressor checking [=meta/file_output=] or streaming the outputs to the files using them.
</div>
[=formula/formula_data=] is [=checksummed Zstandard data=] ([[#shared-external-structure]]).

<div class=note>
  Formula blocks always use Zstandard, which can still store data verbatim with raw Zstandard blocks.
  Many small formula blocks are expected to share a dictionary to compensate for the lack of shared compression context.
</div>

A formula block describes the content of a file as an expression over [=non-leaf items=] following it,
evaluated with modules and resources preceding it.
The internal structure of [=formula/formula_data=] is described in Formula data ([[#formula-data]]).

</div>

<h3 id=formula-data>
Formula data</h3>

This section describes the internal structure of [=formula/formula_data=] in formula blocks ([[#formula-block]]),
after it has been decompressed.

A formula consists of the lists of blocks it references, followed by [=formula/instructions=] of a stack machine.
Each instruction pops zero or more byte sequences from the stack and pushes zero or more byte sequences,
and the byte sequences remaining on the stack after the last instruction are the outputs of the formula.

<div dfn-for=formula>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_blocks</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_ordinal_deltas</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/num_blocks=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_resources</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>resource_indices</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/num_resources=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_modules</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>module_indices</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/num_modules=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>instructions</dfn>
      <td>[=formula/instruction=][*]
  </tbody>
</table>

[=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=] lists the [=non-leaf items=] referenced by the formula block.
Despite their names, [=formula/num_blocks=], [=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=] and [=formula/slice_block=] count non-leaf items,
which are blocks except for [=import/import|imports=].
Their <em>0-based</em> ordinals among the non-leaf items supplied by the non-leaf blocks following the formula block
within the [=segment=], in the order of appearance,
are |o|<sub>0</sub> = [=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=][0] and
|o|<sub>|i|</sub> = |o|<sub>|i| - 1</sub> + [=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=][|i|] + 1 for |i| ≥ 1,
so the ordinals are strictly increasing.
The |i|-th <dfn>referenced item</dfn> is the non-leaf item with the ordinal |o|<sub>|i|</sub>, which MUST exist.

[=formula/resource_indices=] and [=formula/module_indices=] list the resource and module blocks
referenced by the formula block, as <em>0-based</em> indices within the [=segment=] ([[#content-blocks]]).
Each of them MUST refer to an existing block, which precedes the formula block by [[#block-order]].

[=formula/instructions=] fill the rest of [=formula/formula_data=].
Each item or block listed in the above lists MUST be used by at least one instruction,
and is said to be referenced by the formula block only in that case.

<div class=note>
  Non-leaf items are referenced by ordinals rather than by offsets,
  so that relocating or recompressing a block, or inserting other blocks such as mirror blocks,
  doesn't invalidate formula blocks referencing blocks after it.
  An offset would also make the result depend on how the block is located:
  an offset pointing into the middle of a block can still find a valid-looking block there,
  e.g. in an archive stored inside an uncompressed block,
  while a decompressor reading blocks in order would find no block at that offset.
  Ordinals in a typical parcel are consecutive, so [=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=] are mostly zero.
  Resources and modules are referenced by absolute indices,
  which recur in many formula blocks and compress well with a shared dictionary.
</div>

Each <dfn>instruction</dfn> consists of an <dfn>opcode</dfn> of type [=u8=] followed by its operands,
whose structure is determined by the opcode as described in the following sections.
[=formula/opcode=] MUST be one of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Opcode
      <th>Instruction
      <th>Pops
      <th>Pushes
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>00<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-nop|Nop]]
      <td>0
      <td>0
    <tr>
      <td>01<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-literal|Literal]]
      <td>0
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>0F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-swap|Swap]]
      <td>0
      <td>0
    <tr>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-slice|Slice]]
      <td>0
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>11<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-apply|Apply]]
      <td>[=formula/apply_num_inputs=]
      <td>[=formula/apply_num_outputs=]
    <tr>
      <td>12<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-concat|Concatenate]]
      <td>[=formula/concat_count=]
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>13<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-pack|Pack]]
      <td>[=formula/pack_count=]
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>14<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-split|Split]]
      <td>1
      <td>[=formula/split_count=]
    <tr>
      <td>15<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-patch|Patch]]
      <td>2
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>20<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-checksum|Checksum]]
      <td>0
      <td>0
  </tbody>
</table>

Opcodes are grouped by their roles:
00<sub>16</sub> through 0F<sub>16</sub> push data in the formula itself or rearrange the stack,
10<sub>16</sub> through 1F<sub>16</sub> compute byte sequences from [=referenced items=] or the stack,
and 20<sub>16</sub> through 2F<sub>16</sub> check them.
Other opcodes are reserved.

Issue: A reference to a preceding block in the same segment, e.g. to patch a file stored earlier in the segment, is not yet defined.
A reference to another file is made by an [=import/import=] ([[#import-block]]) rather than by an instruction,
and the content of a file in preceding segments by an [=internal import=].
The candidate is a second list of [=non-leaf items=] preceding the formula block, read by Slice instructions as [=referenced items=] are,
where the instructions of another formula block are copied when its output is needed, instead of referring to the output.
It requires a bound on the distance of such a reference declared before the referenced blocks,
so that a streaming decompressor knows which blocks to retain,
and a way to locate a preceding block without reading the segment from its beginning (`docs/xform-design.md` §7).

[=formula/formula_data=] is not randomly accessible,
and a decompressor is expected to read and decompress it in its entirety before evaluating any part of the formula.
Every requirement on [=formula/formula_data=] that doesn't depend on the evaluated byte sequences,
such as indices into the lists and the number of byte sequences on the stack,
can be checked in a single pass over [=formula/instructions=] without evaluating anything.
The same pass determines the length of every byte sequence on the stack,
so [=formula/formula_data=] then serves as an index for random access within the output,
in the same way as a seek table of other formats.
The [=referenced items=] are located by skipping blocks following the formula block by their [=block/size=] fields,
and an item of an import block by decoding that block.

<div class=note>
  The index grows linearly with the number of non-leaf items referenced,
  but it compresses well because most instructions share the same shape and sizes.
  The decompression of the whole [=formula/formula_data=] is a one-time cost per file.
</div>

To <dfn>execute an instruction</dfn> on a stack, which is a [=list=] of [=byte sequences=] whose last item is the top,
perform the steps described for its [=formula/opcode=] in the following sections.
To pop |n| items, the stack MUST have at least |n| items,
and the last |n| items are removed from the stack and used in their order in the stack.

<h4 id=insn-nop>
Nop instruction</h4>

Opcode 00<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Nop instruction**, which has no operands and does nothing.

<h4 id=insn-literal>
Literal instruction</h4>

Opcode 01<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Literal instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>literal_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>literal_data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=formula/literal_size=]]
  </tbody>
</table>

It pushes [=formula/literal_data=].

<h4 id=insn-swap>
Swap instruction</h4>

Opcode 0F<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Swap instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>swap_depth</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The stack MUST have more than [=formula/swap_depth=] items.
It exchanges the top of the stack with the item [=formula/swap_depth=] items below it.

<h4 id=insn-slice>
Slice instruction</h4>

Opcode 10<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Slice instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>slice_block</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>slice_start</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>slice_length</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
  </tbody>
</table>

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. [=formula/slice_block=] MUST be less than [=formula/num_blocks=].
    2. Let |c| be the [=stored content=] of the [=formula/slice_block=]-th [=referenced item=].
    3. [=formula/slice_start=] + [=formula/slice_length=] MUST be at most the length of |c|.
    4. Push the bytes of |c| in [[=formula/slice_start=], [=formula/slice_start=] + [=formula/slice_length=]).
</ol>

<h4 id=insn-apply>
Apply instruction</h4>

Opcode 11<sub>16</sub> denotes an **Apply instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_module</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_num_resources</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_resources</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/apply_num_resources=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_params_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_params</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=formula/apply_params_size=]]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_memory_limit</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_num_inputs</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_num_outputs</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>apply_output_sizes</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/apply_num_outputs=]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. [=formula/apply_module=] MUST be less than [=formula/num_modules=],
        and each item of [=formula/apply_resources=] MUST be less than [=formula/num_resources=].
    2. [=formula/apply_num_outputs=] MUST be at least 1.
    3. Pop [=formula/apply_num_inputs=] items as |inputs|.
    4. Let |outputs| be the result of [=apply a module|applying=]
        the module block at [=formula/module_indices=][[=formula/apply_module=]]
        with |inputs|, the resource blocks at [=formula/resource_indices=][|r|] for each |r| of [=formula/apply_resources=],
        [=formula/apply_params=], [=formula/apply_output_sizes=] and [=formula/apply_memory_limit=] ([[#module-execution]]).
    5. Push each item of |outputs| in order.
</ol>

<h4 id=insn-concat>
Concatenate instruction</h4>

Opcode 12<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Concatenate instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>concat_count</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
  </tbody>
</table>

It pops [=formula/concat_count=] items and pushes their concatenation in order.

<h4 id=insn-pack>
Pack instruction</h4>

Opcode 13<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Pack instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>pack_count</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
  </tbody>
</table>

It pops [=formula/pack_count=] items and pushes the [=packed byte sequence=] of them in order ([[#packed-byte-sequences]]).

<h4 id=insn-split>
Split instruction</h4>

Opcode 14<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Split instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>split_count</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>split_sizes</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/split_count=]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. [=formula/split_count=] MUST be at least 1.
    2. Pop an item |x|. The sum of [=formula/split_sizes=] MUST be equal to the length of |x|.
    3. For each |n| of [=formula/split_sizes=] in order, push the next |n| bytes of |x|.
</ol>

<h4 id=insn-patch>
Patch instruction</h4>

Opcode 15<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Patch instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>patch_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
  </tbody>
</table>

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. Pop 2 items as |source| and |patch|.
    2. |patch| MUST consist of one or more [=Zstandard blocks=] without any other bytes,
        where only the last block is signalled as the final block by its block header,
        and each block holds at most 128 KiB of data.
    3. Let |output| be the result of decompressing |patch| as a sequence of Zstandard blocks [[!RFC8878]]
        with |source| as the `Content` of a raw content dictionary,
        i.e. the entropy tables start empty and the repeat offsets start at their initial values (1, 4, 8).
        The offset of every match MUST be at most the length of |source| plus the number of bytes already decompressed.
    4. The length of |output| MUST be [=formula/patch_size=].
    5. Push |output|.
</ol>

<div class=note>
  A Patch instruction decompresses Zstandard blocks with an earlier byte sequence as a raw content dictionary,
  as `zstd --patch-from` does, so a decompressor reuses its Zstandard decompressor for it.
  The earlier byte sequence is an operand on the stack rather than a [[#dictionary-block|dictionary block]],
  so that it can be any byte sequence computed by the formula,
  e.g. an [=import/import=] or a stream split from it by a module.
  The patch itself is typically a slice of an uncompressed non-leaf data block,
  whose checksum covers the stored patch, while an [=output checksum=] covers the result.
</div>

<div class=note>
  A Patch instruction has no Zstandard frame and therefore no `Window_Size`:
  a match may refer to any byte of |source| or of the output so far,
  and the guaranteed limit of `Window_Size` in [[#zstandard-compression-method]] doesn't apply.
  The decompressor holds both byte sequences anyway, so a long reach costs no additional memory.
  With libzstd, this is decompressing a frame after `ZSTD_DCtx_refPrefix` with |source|,
  whose release builds only check offsets against the start of the prefix;
  a decompressor enforcing `Window_Size` has to lift that check for the frame.
  A compressor using libzstd sets `ZSTD_c_windowLog` large enough to cover |source| and the output,
  as `zstd --patch-from` does.
</div>

<h4 id=insn-checksum>
Checksum instruction</h4>

Opcode 20<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Checksum instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum_value</dfn>
      <td>[=u64le=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The stack MUST NOT be empty.
The [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] checksum of the top of the stack MUST be equal to [=formula/checksum_value=].

A Checksum instruction <dfn>covers</dfn> the byte sequence at the top of the stack when it is executed,
and the byte sequence remains the same item when moved by Swap instructions.
An <dfn>output checksum</dfn> is a Checksum instruction [=covers|covering=] an output of the formula block.

</div>

<h3 id=formula-evaluation>
Formula evaluation</h3>

To <dfn>evaluate a formula block</dfn> |block|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. Let |data| be the [=decoded data=] of [=formula/formula_data=] of |block|, parsed as in [[#formula-data]].
    2. Let |stack| be an empty [=list=] of [=byte sequences=].
    3. For each |insn| of [=formula/instructions=] of |data| in order,
        [=execute an instruction|execute=] |insn| on |stack| as described for its [=formula/opcode=] in [[#formula-data]].
    4. The number of items of |stack| MUST be [=formula/num_outputs=] of |block|.
    5. Return |stack| as the outputs of |block|, numbered from 0 in order from the bottom of the stack.
</ol>

<div class=note>
  Every byte sequence on the stack is used exactly once, as an input of another instruction or as an output,
  so a formula is a tree of instructions rooted at its outputs,
  except that an instruction pushing multiple items, an Apply or a Split instruction, is shared by them.
  Byte sequences are defined as fully evaluated values.
  Lazy, streaming or partial evaluation is an implementation strategy and doesn't change the result
  ([[#partial-access]]).
  A Checksum instruction covering an intermediate byte sequence, such as the output of an Apply instruction,
  can be verified by a decompressor that has computed that byte sequence but not the entire output.
</div>

<h4 id=packed-byte-sequences>
Packed byte sequences</h4>

The <dfn>packed byte sequence</dfn> of a [=list=] of [=byte sequences=] |items|
is the concatenation of the following, where |n| is the number of |items|:

* |n| as [=u64le=];
* For each |i| in [0, |n|), the sum of the lengths of |items|[0] through |items|[|i|] as [=u64le=];
* Each item of |items| in order.

A byte sequence |p| is a packed byte sequence of exactly one [=list=] |items|, if any.
To <dfn>unpack</dfn> |p|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If |p| is shorter than 8 bytes, return failure.
    2. Let |n| be the first 8 bytes of |p| as [=u64le=], and let |h| be 8 × (|n| + 1).
        If |h| exceeds the length of |p|, return failure.
    3. Let |e|<sub>|i|</sub> be the 8 bytes of |p| from the offset 8 × (|i| + 1) as [=u64le=] for each |i| in [0, |n|),
        and let |e|<sub>-1</sub> be 0.
    4. If |e|<sub>|i| - 1</sub> > |e|<sub>|i|</sub> for any |i| in [0, |n|), return failure.
    5. If |h| + |e|<sub>|n| - 1</sub> is not the length of |p|, return failure.
    6. Return the [=list=] of the bytes of |p| in [|h| + |e|<sub>|i| - 1</sub>, |h| + |e|<sub>|i|</sub>) for each |i| in [0, |n|).
</ol>

<div class=note>
  Packed byte sequences let a module take a list of byte sequences whose number varies between applications,
  e.g. one per section of an executable, as a single input,
  since the number of inputs of a module is fixed by its [=entry point=] ([[#module-execution]]).
  The boundaries are not stored: a Pack instruction derives them from the lengths of its items,
  which are known without evaluating anything ([[#formula-data]]).
  A module still has to [=unpack=] its input and fail on a failure,
  since the same bytes can be given in any other way, e.g. by a Literal instruction followed by a Concatenate instruction.
  The fixed-width boundaries let a module find any item without parsing the others,
  and precede the items so that a [=part module=] can request them first.

  In the other direction, a module producing a list of byte sequences outputs their plain concatenation,
  which a Split instruction separates by the sizes declared in the formula.
  The output of such a module is passed to another module taking a packed byte sequence
  by a Split instruction followed by a Pack instruction.
</div>

<h2 id=modules>
Modules</h2>

<h3 id=module-block>
Module block</h3>

Blocks 60<sub>16</sub> through 63<sub>16</sub> are **module blocks**,
whose [=block/type=] is <code>0110&thinsp;00|ss|</code> in binary ([[#block-types]]).
Their data is [=checksummed Zstandard data=] ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
except that block 60<sub>16</sub> is a [=shorthand block=] for a [=registered module=] ([[#registered-blocks]]).

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<div dfn-for=module>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>wasm_module</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
  </tbody>
</table>

[=module/wasm_module=] is a WebAssembly module in the binary format [[!WASM-CORE-3]],
i.e. the content of an ordinary `.wasm` file, starting with the magic `00 61 73 6D` and the version `01 00 00 00`
(hexadecimal) and spanning the whole decoded data.
It MUST decode and validate as a module of the features allowed in [[#module-execution]],
and MUST conform to the profile and the interface given there;
otherwise an [=invalid archive=] failure occurs.
In short, the module defines no memory of its own and imports every memory and host function from the module name `aqr`,
has no start function, and exports a single [=entry point=] that the host invokes once per application.
Custom sections are allowed and don't affect the result.
The format adds nothing around the module, so that the decoded data can be inspected and run by any WebAssembly tool,
and a module block for a [=registered module=] has the same [=module/wasm_module=] as the entry.

The <dfn>module hash</dfn> of a module block is the SHA-512/256 hash [[!FIPS180-4]] of its [=module/wasm_module=],
which is also the [=registered hash=] of a [=registered module=] ([[#registered-blocks]]).
Since every application of a module depends only on [=module/wasm_module=] and its arguments ([[#module-execution]]),
a decompressor MAY apply a module whose [=module hash=] it knows
with an implementation of its own (e.g. native code) instead of instantiating [=module/wasm_module=],
as long as the result is indistinguishable from the definition, including [=invalid archive=] failures.
Such a decompressor needs neither the bytes of the module nor a WebAssembly engine,
whether the module is given by a [=shorthand block=] or in full.
A decompressor MAY refuse to apply a module as its [=conformance level=] allows ([[#conformance-levels]]),
in which case a [=resource failure=] occurs.

<div class=note>
  A compressor can use a [=shorthand block=] for a [=registered module=] to save the size of the module,
  at the cost of an [=unsupported failure=] in a decompressor that doesn't know the entry.
  A module given in full can be read by any decompressor with a WebAssembly engine.
</div>

<div class=note>
  A decompressor chooses how to apply each module, and none of the choices requires translating modules to native code.
  For a module whose [=module hash=] it knows, such as a [=registered module=] at [=level 1=], it can either:

  * apply an implementation of its own, which is the fastest but has to be made for every module known.
      Translating the module by automated means (e.g. wasm2c) is preferred to writing it anew,
      since the result has to be indistinguishable from the module, including its failures; or
  * instantiate the module with a WebAssembly engine without isolating it,
      since the hash is strong enough that no other module can have it.
      This needs only the hash for a module given in full, and the bytes only for a [=shorthand block=].

  For a module whose [=module hash=] it doesn't know, it can either:

  * refuse to apply it, failing with a [=resource failure=], unless it is at [=level 2=]; or
  * instantiate it with a WebAssembly engine in a sandbox, as untrusted code ([[#security-considerations]]).

  For example, a decompressor at [=level 1=] needs neither an engine nor a sandbox if it implements every registered module natively,
  and one at [=level 2=] needs both but no native implementation.
</div>

</div>

<h3 id=resource-block>
Resource block</h3>

Blocks 64<sub>16</sub> through 67<sub>16</sub> are **resource blocks**,
whose [=block/type=] is <code>0110&thinsp;01|ss|</code> in binary ([[#block-types]]).
Their data is [=checksummed Zstandard data=] ([[#shared-external-structure]]).

The internal structure after decompression is an uninterpreted byte sequence,
which is supplied to modules by formula blocks referencing it ([[#formula-data]], [[#module-execution]]).

<h3 id=module-execution>
Module execution</h3>

Issue: This section is tentative and follows `docs/xform-design.md` §5.

A module is a WebAssembly module [[!WASM-CORE-3]] stored as [=module/wasm_module=],
instantiated and invoked through the embedding interface defined in the Appendix of [[!WASM-CORE-3]].
It MUST conform to the following profile, or an [=invalid archive=] failure occurs:

* WebAssembly 3.0 core, executed under the deterministic profile;
    every NaN result is a canonical NaN.
* None of the features added in WebAssembly 3.0 are allowed,
    except multiple memories and 64-bit addresses of the memories other than the [=working memory=].
    That is, tail calls, extended constant expressions, typed function references, garbage collection,
    exception handling, relaxed SIMD, 64-bit tables and a 64-bit [=working memory=] are not allowed.
* Imported tables are not allowed.
* It has no start function.
* It defines no memory, and imports each memory at most once.
* Every `memory.grow` instruction targets the [=working memory=].
* The import type of every memory other than the [=working memory=] has 64-bit addresses, the minimum 0 and no maximum.
* It exports exactly one of the functions `aqr_apply_whole` and `aqr_apply_part`, the <dfn>entry point</dfn>.
    The module is a <dfn>whole module</dfn> in the former case and a <dfn>part module</dfn> in the latter.
* If it imports [=aqr_spawn|`aqr_spawn`=], it exports the function `aqr_task`, the <dfn>task entry point</dfn>,
    which has two parameters of type `i32` and no results,
    and it has exactly one custom section named `aqr_task_slots`, whose content is one byte from 1 to 64, its [=task slots=].
    Otherwise it has no custom section of that name.

Issue: Whether the guaranteed limit of 2<sup>27</sup> bytes (128 MiB) for the Zstandard `Window_Size` ([[#zstandard-compression-method]])
should also apply to the memory limit.

Every import of a module is from the module name `aqr`, under one of the following names.
The names of imports and exports are prefixed with `aqr_` to avoid collisions in the toolchains and hosts
that link them into one namespace.
Any other import is an [=invalid archive=] failure.

: `aqr_memory`
:: A memory, the <dfn>working memory</dfn>.
: `aqr_params`
:: A memory of the parameters.
: `aqr_resource`|i|
:: A memory of the |i|-th resource, where |i| is a decimal number without leading zeros.
: `aqr_input`|k|
:: A memory of the |k|-th input, where |k| is a decimal number without leading zeros.
: `aqr_output`|j|
:: A memory of the |j|-th output, where |j| is a decimal number without leading zeros.
: [=aqr_request|`aqr_request`=], [=aqr_release|`aqr_release`=], [=aqr_commit|`aqr_commit`=]
:: Functions of a [=part module=], defined below. A [=whole module=] MUST NOT import them.
: [=aqr_heartbeat|`aqr_heartbeat`=]
:: A function defined below.
: [=aqr_spawn|`aqr_spawn`=], [=aqr_result|`aqr_result`=], [=aqr_join|`aqr_join`=], [=aqr_take|`aqr_take`=]
:: Functions of [=tasks=], defined in [[#tasks]].
: [=aqr_chan|`aqr_chan`=], [=aqr_send|`aqr_send`=], [=aqr_recv|`aqr_recv`=], [=aqr_close|`aqr_close`=]
:: Functions of [=channels=], defined in [[#tasks]].
: [=aqr_log|`aqr_log`=]
:: The diagnostic function, defined below.

A memory is <dfn>sized to fit</dfn> a length |L| when both its minimum and maximum are &lceil;|L| / 65536&rceil; pages.

To <dfn>apply a module</dfn> |module| given a [=list=] of input [=byte sequences=] |inputs|,
a [=list=] of resource blocks |resources|, a parameter [=byte sequence=] |params|,
a [=list=] of declared output sizes |sizes| and a memory limit:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. |sizes| MUST NOT be empty.
    2. Every `aqr_resource`|i| imported MUST satisfy |i| < the number of |resources|,
        every `aqr_input`|k| imported MUST satisfy |k| < the number of |inputs|,
        and every `aqr_output`|j| imported MUST satisfy |j| < the number of |sizes|.
    3. Create the memories imported, each initially zero-filled:
        * The [=working memory=] has the minimum size of its import type.
        * The memory of the parameters is [=sized to fit=] the length of |params|, and holds |params| from offset 0.
        * The memory of the |i|-th resource is [=sized to fit=] its [=stored content=] |c|, and holds |c| from offset 0.
        * The memory of the |k|-th input is [=sized to fit=] the length of |inputs|[|k|].
            If |module| is a [=whole module=], it holds |inputs|[|k|] from offset 0.
            Otherwise let |W|<sub>|k|</sub> be its size in bytes and the <dfn>visible range</dfn> of the |k|-th input be [0, 0).
        * The memory of the |j|-th output is [=sized to fit=] |sizes|[|j|].
            If |module| is a [=part module=], let |W|<sub>out,|j|</sub> be its size in bytes.
    4. Let |outputs| be a [=list=] of as many empty [=byte sequences=] as |sizes|.
    5. If the total size of the memories in bytes exceeds the memory limit, return a [=resource failure=].
        The memory limit applies to the total size of the memories of the application, those of its [=tasks=] included,
        and of the messages queued in its [=channels=].
    6. Instantiate |module| with the memories and the host functions below.
    7. Invoke the [=entry point=] with the arguments of type `i64`, in order:
        the length of |params|, the length of the [=stored content=] of each item of |resources|,
        the length of each item of |inputs| and each item of |sizes|.
        The [=entry point=] MUST have these parameters and no results.
        If it traps or any host function below signals an error, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
        If it exceeds the memory limit or any implementation-defined limit, return a [=resource failure=].
        If any [=task=] of the application fails, return its failure (that of any of them if several fail).
        If every instance of the application [=waits=], return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
        Once it returns, every [=task=] it spawned MUST be [=task/taken=].
    8. If |module| is a [=whole module=], set each |outputs|[|j|] to the first |sizes|[|j|] bytes of the memory of the |j|-th output,
        if imported, and to |sizes|[|j|] zero bytes otherwise.
    9. The length of each |outputs|[|j|] MUST be |sizes|[|j|].
    10. Return |outputs|.
</ol>

Issue: Windows smaller than their data for a [=part module=], i.e. ring buffers,
which would allow import types with a maximum.

A `memory.grow` instruction exceeds the memory limit when the total size of the memories of the application,
those of its [=tasks=] and the messages queued in its [=channels=] included, would exceed it,
and MUST succeed otherwise if the maximum of the import type of the [=working memory=] allows.

<div class=note>
  Each application instantiates the module anew, so nothing remains from an earlier one.
  The size of every memory except the working memory is fixed by the length of its data,
  so the declarations of an application need no windows.
  A module needing a window that depends on its data keeps the bytes in the working memory.
  The memory of an output of a [=whole module=] can be passed to the module consuming it
  as the memory of a [=whole module=]'s input, as long as the bytes from offset |sizes|[|j|] are zeroed,
  since the result is the same.
</div>

Host functions are defined as follows, where |lo|<sub>|k|</sub> and |hi|<sub>|k|</sub> denote the [=visible range=] of the |k|-th input,
|k|, |j|, |ptr| and |len| are of type `i32`, and every offset is of type `i64`.
Called by a [=task=], [=aqr_request=], [=aqr_release=] and [=aqr_commit=] signal an error.

: <dfn>aqr_request</dfn>(|k|, |hi|)
:: The |k|-th input MUST be imported.
    |hi| MUST be in [|hi|<sub>|k|</sub>, length of |inputs|[|k|]], and |hi| - |lo|<sub>|k|</sub> MUST be at most |W|<sub>|k|</sub>.
    For each offset |o| in [|hi|<sub>|k|</sub>, |hi|), write |inputs|[|k|][|o|] to the memory of the |k|-th input at |o| % |W|<sub>|k|</sub>.
    Then set |hi|<sub>|k|</sub> to |hi|.
: <dfn>aqr_release</dfn>(|k|, |lo|)
:: The |k|-th input MUST be imported.
    |lo| MUST be in [|lo|<sub>|k|</sub>, |hi|<sub>|k|</sub>]. Set |lo|<sub>|k|</sub> to |lo|.
: <dfn>aqr_commit</dfn>(|j|, |hi|)
:: The |j|-th output MUST be imported.
    |hi| MUST be in [length of |outputs|[|j|], |sizes|[|j|]], and |hi| - length of |outputs|[|j|] MUST be at most |W|<sub>out,|j|</sub>.
    For each offset |o| in [length of |outputs|[|j|], |hi|), append the byte at |o| % |W|<sub>out,|j|</sub> of the memory of the |j|-th output
    to |outputs|[|j|].
: <dfn>aqr_heartbeat</dfn>()
:: Does nothing.
: <dfn>aqr_log</dfn>(|ptr|, |len|)
:: Does nothing.
    Implementations MAY present the |len| bytes of the [=working memory=] from the offset |ptr| as a diagnostic message,
    which is untrusted data from the archive, but doing so MUST NOT affect the application,
    even when the range is out of bounds.
    It is the only diagnostic function; a module reports a failure by `aqr_log` followed by a trap.

A violation of any MUST in host functions is signalled as an error to the module invocation.

<div class=note>
  The host writes input memories of a [=part module=] only within [=aqr_request=] and reads its output memories only within [=aqr_commit=],
  so the content of every memory depends only on the order of calls made by the module.
  Implementations MAY block in [=aqr_request=] until the requested bytes are available
  and in [=aqr_commit=] until consumers catch up,
  as long as the result is indistinguishable from this definition.
</div>

<h4 id=tasks>
Tasks</h4>

A <dfn>task</dfn> is an instance of the module of its own,
run on a copy of the [=working memory=] of the instance that spawns it and giving back a [=byte sequence=], its <dfn for=task>result</dfn>.
The application's instance and every task are the instances of the application;
each instance has a [=list=] of handles, initially empty, each of which is a [=list=] of the tasks spawned at once.
A task is either pending, running, done (with a result), failed (with an [=invalid archive=] failure or a [=resource failure=]),
or <dfn for=task>taken</dfn>.

Of the tasks an instance spawns, at most as many as the module's <dfn>task slots</dfn> (its custom section `aqr_task_slots`) are running at once.
A task is pending from its spawn until it is started; whenever fewer of the tasks its instance spawned are running than the [=task slots=],
the first of them pending, in the order they were spawned (by handle, then by index), is started:
it is running from then, and [=run a task|runs=].
A running task stops running when it is done or failed.
A pending task is not yet an instance of the application: it has no memories, and it does not [=wait=].

A <dfn>channel</dfn> links instances of an application: a [=queue=] of messages ([=byte sequences=]) of at most its <dfn for=channel>capacity</dfn>,
whether it is <dfn for=channel>closed</dfn>, and its <dfn for=channel>sender</dfn> and <dfn for=channel>receiver</dfn>,
instances initially none.
The application has a [=list=] of channels, initially empty.
When an instance ends (returns or fails), every channel of which it is the sender is closed.
An instance <dfn>waits</dfn> while it is within [=aqr_join=], [=aqr_send=] or [=aqr_recv=] and the condition it waits for does not hold.

To <dfn>run a task</dfn> given a [=byte sequence=] |S|, |arg| and |i|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. Create the memories imported:
        the [=working memory=] has |S|.length / 65536 pages and the maximum of its import type,
        and every other memory has the minimum and maximum 0.
    2. Instantiate the module with the memories and the host functions, and then set the content of the working memory to |S|.
    3. Invoke the [=task entry point=] with |arg| and |i|.
        If it traps or any host function signals an error, the task fails with an [=invalid archive=] failure.
        If it exceeds the memory limit or any implementation-defined limit, the task fails with a [=resource failure=].
    4. Once it returns, every task it spawned MUST be [=task/taken=], or the task fails with an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    5. The task is done, with the result last set by [=aqr_result=], or an empty [=byte sequence=] if none.
</ol>

The host functions of tasks are defined as follows, for the instance |I| calling them,
where |arg|, |n|, |h|, |i|, |ptr|, |dst| and |len| are of type `i32`, unsigned:

: <dfn>aqr_spawn</dfn>(|arg|, |n|)
:: Let |S| be the content of the [=working memory=] of |I|.
    Append to the handles of |I| a [=list=] of |n| new pending tasks, the |i|-th (from 0) of which is to [=run a task=] given |S|, |arg| and |i|
    once it is started (as the [=task slots=] allow), and return the index of that list among the handles of |I|.
: <dfn>aqr_result</dfn>(|ptr|, |len|)
:: |I| MUST be a task, and |ptr| + |len| MUST be at most the size of its [=working memory=].
    Set the result of |I| to the |len| bytes of its [=working memory=] from the offset |ptr|.
: <dfn>aqr_join</dfn>(|h|, |i|)
:: The |i|-th task of the |h|-th handle of |I| MUST exist and not be [=task/taken=].
    Wait until it is done or failed.
    If it failed, the instance |I| fails with its failure.
    Otherwise return the length of its result.
: <dfn>aqr_take</dfn>(|h|, |i|, |dst|, |len|)
:: The |i|-th task of the |h|-th handle of |I| MUST be done (joined),
    |len| MUST be at most the length of its result,
    and |dst| + |len| MUST be at most the size of the [=working memory=] of |I|.
    Copy the first |len| bytes of its result to the [=working memory=] of |I| from the offset |dst|, and the task is [=task/taken=].

The host functions of channels are defined as follows, where |c|, |ptr|, |dst|, |len| and |cap| are of type `i32`, unsigned,
the channel |C| is the |c|-th (from 0) of the application, which MUST exist,
and taking an end of |C| for |I| means: if that end is none, set it to |I|; otherwise it MUST be |I|.

: <dfn>aqr_chan</dfn>(|cap|)
:: |cap| MUST be at least 1.
    Append to the channels of the application a new channel of [=channel/capacity=] |cap|, and return its index.
: <dfn>aqr_send</dfn>(|c|, |ptr|, |len|)
:: Take the [=channel/sender=] of |C| for |I|.
    |C| MUST NOT be [=channel/closed=], and |ptr| + |len| MUST be at most the size of the [=working memory=] of |I|.
    Wait until fewer messages than the [=channel/capacity=] of |C| are queued,
    and then enqueue the |len| bytes of the [=working memory=] of |I| from the offset |ptr|.
: <dfn>aqr_recv</dfn>(|c|, |dst|, |cap|)
:: Take the [=channel/receiver=] of |C| for |I|.
    Wait until a message is queued or |C| is [=channel/closed=].
    If no message is queued, return &minus;1 (0xFFFFFFFF).
    Otherwise let |M| be the first message.
    If the length of |M| exceeds |cap|, return it, leaving |M| queued.
    Otherwise |dst| + the length of |M| MUST be at most the size of the [=working memory=] of |I|;
    dequeue |M|, copy it to the [=working memory=] of |I| from the offset |dst|, and return its length.
: <dfn>aqr_close</dfn>(|c|)
:: Take the [=channel/sender=] of |C| for |I|, and close |C|.

<div class=note>
  A task shares nothing with the instance that spawned it but the copy of the working memory it starts with, the result it gives back
  and the messages of the channels whose ends they are,
  and each end of a channel is an instance's: the instances are a Kahn process network,
  so the outputs do not depend on when, in what order or how many at once the tasks run.
  Implementations MAY run a task at any time from its start and on any thread,
  as long as an instance waiting does not keep the others from running.
  The [=task slots=] bound the memory of an application, its memory limit included:
  a pending task holds no memory under the limit (what implementations keep of |S| for it is their own),
  and an application that needs more of an instance's tasks at once than the [=task slots=], such as a pipeline of more stages,
  is an [=invalid archive=] whenever its running instances all wait for one that is pending.
  The tasks an instance spawns start in the order it spawned them, whatever the others do,
  so whether that happens does not depend on when the tasks run either.
  A channel is closed by its sender ending only if it sent to it or closed it,
  so an instance that may send no message to a channel closes it itself.
  Every memory but the working memory is empty to a task, so the data a task works on is in the working memory;
  its globals are those of a new instance.
  The tasks of a spawn start from the same copy, which implementations MAY share between them by copying on write.
  Whether the memory limit is exceeded may depend on how many tasks are running at once, which only decides a [=resource failure=].
</div>

Issue: Whether the memories other than the working memory, read-only, should be visible to tasks,
which would require rejecting the instructions writing them; and the windows of a [=part module=] in tasks.


<h2 id=overlays>
Overlay archives and imports</h2>

<h3 id=import-block>
Import block</h3>

Blocks 58<sub>16</sub> through 5F<sub>16</sub> are **import blocks**,
which are [=non-leaf blocks=] supplying byte ranges of files outside the [=segment=].
Their [=block/type=] is <code>0101&thinsp;1|e||ss|</code> in binary ([[#block-types]]),
and their data is [=checksummed Zstandard data=] ([[#shared-external-structure]]).

<div dfn-for=import>

|e| determines the source (<dfn>external</dfn>): preceding segments if 0, and the [=base=] if 1.

An import block is an <dfn>internal import block</dfn> if [=import/external=] is false,
and an <dfn>external import block</dfn> otherwise.
An external import block MUST NOT occur in an archive other than an [=overlay archive=] ([[#unencrypted-archive]]).

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_imports</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ 0
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>imports</dfn>
      <td>[=import/import=][[=import/num_imports=]]
  </tbody>
</table>

Each <dfn>import</dfn> consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>import_path</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*] ≠ 0
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>import_path_sentinel</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>import_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>import_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>import_checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
  </tbody>
</table>

An import in an [=internal import block|internal=] or [=external import block=]
is an <dfn>internal import</dfn> or an <dfn>external import</dfn> respectively.
Each import supplies a single [=non-leaf item=],
so the import block supplies [=import/num_imports=] items in the order of [=import/imports=].
The stored content of the item is the [=imported content=] of the import, given by [=resolve an import|resolving=] it,
the bytes in [[=import/import_offset=], [=import/import_offset=] + [=import/import_size=])
of the file found at [=import/import_path=]:

* An internal import finds the file in the [=preceding result=] of its segment,
    i.e. the result of applying all preceding segments to the base, which is empty for a [=standalone archive=].
* An external import finds the file in the [=base=] as given.

[=import/import_path=] is a sequence of zero or more components separated by 2F<sub>16</sub> (`/`).
Each component uses the same encodings as [=meta/entry_name=],
and its decoded name is subject to the same restrictions as an [=entry name=], so no component can be empty.
An empty [=import/import_path=] denotes the root itself, which is a file when the base is a single file.

[=import/import_checksum=] is the SHA-512/256 hash [[!FIPS180-4]] of the [=imported content=].

<div class=note>
  Since an [=entry name=] can't contain `/` or be `.` or `..`, splitting [=import/import_path=] is unambiguous,
  and no path can refer to anything outside the root.
  A path is relative to the root rather than to the directory of the file using it,
  because a formula block referencing an import is evaluated before the metadata block names that file,
  and a single leaf block may be shared by files in different directories.
  The prefixes shared by paths in the same block compress well.
</div>

<div class=note>
  An import refers to a byte range rather than a whole file,
  so that a decompressor reading a part of a large file only reads and verifies the part actually imported.
  Multiple ranges of the same file are separate imports.
</div>

<div class=note>
  [=import/import_checksum=] tells a wrong or modified base before anything is evaluated from it.
  This matters because a wrong base often doesn't make the evaluation fail,
  e.g. a [[#insn-patch|Patch instruction]] produces a different output from a different source.
  Unlike other checksums in this specification, it uses a cryptographic hash:
  the base is not part of the archive,
  so an archive authenticated by other means (e.g. a signature) would otherwise not authenticate the result.
  It costs 32 bytes per import, which don't compress.
</div>

<div class=note>
  An import is often used through a module splitting the imported content into streams,
  i.e. the preprocessing part of a transformation,
  so that only the streams that changed have to be patched before the module joining them back is applied.
  Such a module only has to perform the split decided by the compressor, given as its parameters.
</div>

</div>

<h3 id=import-resolution>
Resolving an import</h3>

To <dfn>resolve an import</dfn> |import| of an import block in a [=segment=]:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. Split |import|.[=import/import_path=] at every 2F<sub>16</sub> into components, unless it is empty,
        and let |names| be the result of [=decode an entry name|decoding=] each component as an entry name.
        If any component fails to decode, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    2. Let |node| be the [=preceding result=] of the segment if |import| is an [=internal import=],
        or the [=archive value/root=] of the [=archive base=] otherwise.
    3. For each |name| of |names| in order:
        1. |node| MUST be a [=directory node=], and |node|.[=directory node/children=][|name|] MUST [=map/exist=].
        2. Set |node| to |node|.[=directory node/children=][|name|].
    4. |node| MUST be a [=file node=].
    5. |import|.[=import/import_offset=] + |import|.[=import/import_size=] MUST be at most the length of |node|.[=file node/content=].
    6. Let |content| be the bytes of |node|.[=file node/content=]
        in [|import|.[=import/import_offset=], |import|.[=import/import_offset=] + |import|.[=import/import_size=]).
    7. The SHA-512/256 hash [[!FIPS180-4]] of |content| MUST be equal to |import|.[=import/import_checksum=].
    8. Return |content| as the <dfn>imported content</dfn> of |import|.
</ol>

A violation of any MUST in this algorithm is a [=base failure=] in an [=overlay archive=],
and an [=invalid archive=] failure in a [=standalone archive=].

<div class=note>
  As any other lookup in an archive value, resolving an import never follows a [=link node=],
  since a link node is not a directory node.
  In a standalone archive the preceding result is determined by the archive alone,
  so a failure is a property of the archive.
  In an overlay archive it depends on the base even for an internal import,
  whose path may lead into the part of the base no preceding segment has replaced.
</div>

<h3 id=bases>
Reading a base</h3>

Reading a [=base=] from a file system is outside the archive model, as is extraction.
A decompressor doing so maps a directory or a single file to an [=archive value=], and is subject to the following requirements:

* It MUST NOT use a base unless explicitly given one,
    and in particular MUST NOT use the current directory or the destination directory as a base by default.
* It MUST NOT read anything outside the given directory,
    including through symbolic links or other redirections already present in the base.
    A symbolic link in the base is a [=link node=] and is never followed,
    so an [=import/import=] whose path leads through or to a link fails to resolve ([[#import-resolution]]).
* It MUST NOT read a file other than a regular file as the content of a [=file node=].
    A FIFO or a device file in the base is a [=special node=].
* It SHOULD map a file system name to an [=entry name=] as the reverse of the mapping in [[#extraction]].
    If a name can't be mapped, or multiple names map to the same entry name, it MUST either fail or report it to the user.
* The base MUST appear the same throughout the interpretation, as if it were read at a single point in time.
    A decompressor MAY read only the parts of the base needed by [=import/import|imports=] and by the result,
    and the checksums of imports detect a part that changed while reading.

<div class=note>
  An archive can copy any byte of its base into its result,
  and the [=base failure=] of an import with a guessed content tells whether the guess is right.
  An untrusted archive therefore gets as much access to the base as to its own result,
  which is why the base is only used when explicitly given.
  A decompressor is advised to report the files of the base it has read.
</div>

A decompressor MAY write the result of an [=overlay archive=] into its base directory, i.e. apply it in place.
In that case:

* The result MUST be the same as if every [=imported content=] were read before anything is written,
    e.g. by writing new files under temporary names and renaming them after all of them are written.
* It MAY remove a node of the base only when the node is absent from the result,
    and SHOULD leave every node not given by any segment as it is.

<div class=note>
  Ordering the writes alone is not sufficient in general:
  a file may be patched from another file which is in turn patched from the former,
  e.g. when two files are swapped.
</div>

<h2 id=encrypted-archive>
Encrypted archives</h2>

An archive might have been **encrypted**,
in which case it consists of a block-like header of type [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=] followed by encrypted chunks.
An encrypted archive is <dfn for=envelope>mirrored</dfn> if it starts with [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRRORED_HEADER_MAGIC=],
in which case copies of its header are placed between encrypted chunks.

<div dfn-for=envelope>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][8] ∈ {[=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=], [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRRORED_HEADER_MAGIC=]}
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>lg_work_factor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] ∈ [0C<sub>16</sub>, 3F<sub>16</sub>)
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>scrypt_ephemeral_secret</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][16]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_file_key</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>age_header_mac</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>payload_nonce</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][16]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>header_checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u64le=]
      <td>[=cond/If=] [=envelope/mirrored=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>chunks</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

The 97 bytes from [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] to [=envelope/payload_nonce=] are called the <dfn>header fields</dfn>.

[=envelope/header_checksum=] is the XXH64 [[!XXHASH]] hash with the seed zero
of all bytes from [=envelope/checksum_start=] to [=envelope/checksum_end=].

<div class=note>
  [=envelope/header_checksum=] tells a damaged header from a wrong passphrase before running scrypt.
  Without it, damage to [=envelope/lg_work_factor=], [=envelope/scrypt_ephemeral_secret=] or [=envelope/encrypted_file_key=]
  results in a [=decryption failure=] for any passphrase,
  and damage to [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] may make scrypt run for an arbitrarily long time.
</div>

All fields from [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] to [=envelope/age_header_mac=] are used to reconstruct an age v1 textual file header [[!AGE]] as follows:

<pre>
age-encryption.org/v1
-> scrypt <var>scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64</var> <var>lg_work_factor_decimal</var>
<var>encrypted_file_key_base64</var>
--- <var>age_header_mac_base64</var>
</pre>

Where,

* |scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/scrypt_ephemeral_secret=] without padding ([[!RFC4648]], Section 4);
* |lg_work_factor_decimal| is the decimal representation of [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] without leading zeros;
* |encrypted_file_key_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/encrypted_file_key=] without padding;
* |age_header_mac_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/age_header_mac=] without padding.

The <dfn>age payload</dfn> consists of [=envelope/payload_nonce=] followed by the encrypted chunks in [=envelope/chunks=]
and is interpreted in accordance with the age v1 format.
Every encrypted chunk except the last one is 65,552 bytes long,
i.e. 64 KiB of plaintext followed by a 16-byte authentication tag.

If the archive is not [=envelope/mirrored=], [=envelope/chunks=] consists of the encrypted chunks alone.
Otherwise [=envelope/chunks=] also contains **mirrored headers**:
for each encrypted chunk <var>C<sub>m</sub></var> numbered from 0,
a mirrored header immediately precedes <var>C<sub>m</sub></var>
if and only if <var>m</var> + 1 is either a power of two from 2 to 32 or a positive multiple of 64.
The <var>k</var>-th mirrored header numbered from 1 therefore precedes <var>C<sub>m</sub></var>
where <var>m</var> = 2<sup><var>k</var></sup> − 1 for <var>k</var> ≤ 6 and <var>m</var> = 64(<var>k</var> − 5) − 1 for <var>k</var> ≥ 6,
and no mirrored header follows the last encrypted chunk.
A mirrored header consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=emirror>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>encrypted_mirror_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][8] = [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRROR_MAGIC=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>mirror_index</dfn>
      <td>[=u32le=] = <var>k</var>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>mirrored_fields</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][97] = [=header fields=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>mirror_checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u64le=]
  </tbody>
</table>

[=emirror/mirror_checksum=] is the XXH64 [[!XXHASH]] hash with the seed zero
of all bytes from [=emirror/checksum_start=] to [=emirror/checksum_end=].

As with [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]], the constraints on the fields of a mirrored header are requirements on conforming compressors.
A mirrored header always occupies 117 bytes at its position,
and its content doesn't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=].
A mirrored header is <dfn>intact</dfn> if its [=emirror/mirror_checksum=] verifies
and its fields other than [=emirror/mirrored_fields=] satisfy the constraints above.
A decompressor MUST NOT use the [=emirror/mirrored_fields=] of a mirrored header that is not [=emirror/intact=].

</div>

<div class=note>
  Mirrored headers allow the archive to be decrypted when its header is damaged.
  Since every encrypted chunk except the last one has the same length,
  the <var>k</var>-th mirrored header is always 113 + 65,552<var>m</var> + 117(<var>k</var> − 1) bytes
  after the start of [=envelope/encrypted_header_magic=], with <var>m</var> as above.
  A single mirrored header found by scanning for [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRROR_MAGIC=]
  and verified by [=emirror/mirror_checksum=] thus gives the start of the archive, the position of every encrypted chunk
  and every field needed to decrypt them.
  Unlike [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]], mirrored headers need no offset to tell archives apart:
  [=envelope/scrypt_ephemeral_secret=] and [=envelope/payload_nonce=] are random,
  so mirrored headers with the same [=emirror/mirrored_fields=] belong to the same archive.
</div>

<div class=note>
  The interval between mirrored headers doubles from 1 to 64 encrypted chunks and stays at 64 chunks afterwards,
  which equals the distance of 4 MiB recommended in [[#mirror-block]] measured in plaintext,
  so that small archives have mirrored headers soon after the header
  and the overhead stays at 117 bytes per 64 chunks, about 0.003%, for large archives.
</div>

Issue: The interval of 64 encrypted chunks follows the tentative distance of 4 MiB in [[#mirror-block]] and changes with it (`docs/recovery-design.md` §5.5).

The [=age payload=] MUST decrypt into a valid unencrypted archive ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
either a [=standalone archive=] or an [=overlay archive=],
with the following additional constraints:

* [=archive/size_hint_block=] is no longer optional;
* No spacer block ([[#spacer-block]]) is allowed, in order to discourage an in-place modification of the encrypted archive;
* No trailing bytes are allowed after the archive.

</div>

<h2 id=recovery>
Integrity and recovery</h2>

<h3 id=checksums>
Checksums</h3>

The following table summarizes every checksum defined by this specification:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Structure
      <th>Checksum
      <th>Computed over
      <th>Presence
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>[[#metadata-block|Metadata]], [[#dictionary-block|dictionary]], [[#import-block|import]],
        [[#module-block|module]] and [[#resource-block|resource blocks]], except [=shorthand blocks=]
      <td>XXH64
      <td>[=Decoded data=]
      <td>Always
    <tr>
      <td>[=Shorthand blocks=]
      <td>None (the [=registered hash=] identifies the entry but is not checked)
      <td>
      <td>Never
    <tr>
      <td>Non-leaf [[#data-block|data blocks]]
      <td>XXH64
      <td>[=Decoded data=]
      <td>Always
    <tr>
      <td>Leaf [[#data-block|data blocks]]
      <td>XXH64
      <td>[=Decoded data=]
      <td>By [=block/type=]
    <tr>
      <td>[=formula/formula_data=] of [[#formula-block|formula blocks]]
      <td>XXH64
      <td>[=Decoded data=]
      <td>Always
    <tr>
      <td>Byte sequences in a formula
      <td>XXH64 ([[#insn-checksum|Checksum instruction]])
      <td>The byte sequence [=covers|covered=]
      <td>Optional
    <tr>
      <td>[=import/import|Imports=]
      <td>SHA-512/256 ([=import/import_checksum=])
      <td>[=Imported content=]
      <td>Always
    <tr>
      <td>[[#mirror-block|Mirror blocks]]
      <td>XXH64 ([=mirror/mirror_checksum=])
      <td>Stored bytes
      <td>Always
    <tr>
      <td>Header and mirrored headers of an [[#encrypted-archive|encrypted archive]]
      <td>XXH64 ([=envelope/header_checksum=], [=emirror/mirror_checksum=])
      <td>Stored bytes
      <td>If [=envelope/mirrored=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The encrypted chunks of an encrypted archive are also authenticated by [[AGE]].

Unless explicitly requested otherwise, conforming compressors SHOULD use a [=checksum_method=] other than None
for every leaf block.

Unless explicitly requested otherwise, conforming compressors SHOULD declare an [=output checksum=]
for every formula block.

<div class=note>
  An output checksum is the only checksum verifying a module application or a patch,
  so it matters most for an output computed by an [[#insn-apply|Apply instruction]] or a [[#insn-patch|Patch instruction]].
  An output computed only by other instructions is already covered by the checksums of [=formula/formula_data=]
  and of the [=non-leaf items=] it is computed from,
  but its output checksum still lets the content of the file be verified by itself,
  so that whether every file is covered by a checksum doesn't depend on the instructions of the formula,
  as the [=archival profile=] requires.
</div>

<div class=note>
  A checksum over the stored bytes would only verify that they have been read correctly.
  A checksum over the decoded data also verifies the decompression,
  and lets a decompressor reading only some non-leaf blocks verify the file content it has actually read.
</div>

<div class=note>
  Unlike leaf blocks, the checksum is mandatory for metadata, dictionary, import, module and resource blocks.
  Names and the structure of entries are not covered by any other checksum,
  and the damage to these blocks affects many files at once.
  The cost is negligible because these blocks are a small fraction of the archive and each is read only once.
  It also allows a decompressor to detect the damage to these blocks by reading them alone,
  which is a prerequisite for using [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]].
</div>

<div class=note>
  The checksum is mandatory for non-leaf blocks.
  The output checksum of a formula block covers its entire output,
  so a decompressor that only reads a part of the output can't verify it without reading the whole output.
  Instead it can verify the checksums of non-leaf blocks it has actually read.
  The checksum also lets damage to a non-leaf block be detected before the block is used by a module application,
  so that the damage is always an [=invalid archive=] failure rather than a [=resource failure=],
  as for [=formula/formula_data=] below.
</div>

<div class=note>
  The checksum of [=formula/formula_data=] is mandatory and distinct from the optional [=output checksums=]
  declared inside [=formula/formula_data=] ([[#insn-checksum]]).
  [=formula/formula_data=] is always decoded in its entirety before evaluation,
  so its checksum can be verified even by a decompressor reading only a part of the output.
  It lets a damaged formula block be detected by reading the block alone and before evaluating anything,
  so that a damaged formula block is always an [=invalid archive=] failure
  rather than a [=resource failure=] caused by damaged resource declarations,
  and is distinguished from damage to the non-leaf blocks it references.
  An output checksum, on the other hand, also verifies the evaluation including module applications,
  but can only be verified after computing the entire output.
</div>

<div class=note>
  The checksum is optional for leaf blocks to allow the following:

    * An uncompressed leaf block without a checksum holds the file content verbatim and nothing derived from it,
        so the file can be memory-mapped from the archive and modified in place.
    * A compressed leaf block without a checksum can be made from an existing Zstandard frame by copying its Zstandard blocks,
        without decompressing them to compute the checksum over the decoded data.

  Neither is a concern for long-term preservation, where the [=archival profile=] requires the checksum.
</div>

<h3 id=profiles>
Archival profile</h3>

An archive conforms to the <dfn>archival profile</dfn> when the content of every file is covered by a checksum:

* Every [=leaf block|leaf=] [[#data-block|data block]] has a [=checksum_method=] other than None.
* Every [[#formula-block|formula block]] declares an [=output checksum=].
* The archive is a [=standalone archive=].
* No block is a [=shorthand block=] ([[#registered-blocks]]).

A conforming compressor producing an archive for long-term preservation SHOULD conform to the archival profile.
A tool verifying an archive SHOULD report whether it conforms to the archival profile.

<div class=note>
  Other blocks always have checksums, so an archive conforming to the archival profile
  detects any damage to the content of a file by its decompression alone.
  Whether an archive conforms can be determined from block types and formula blocks, without decompressing data blocks.
  An archive conforming to the archival profile depends on nothing outside itself, neither a [=base=] nor the registries,
  so that it can be read by a decompressor at [=level 2=] ([[#conformance-levels]]) that knows no registry entry.
  It may still use modules not registered,
  which a decompressor at a lower level may refuse to apply.
</div>

<h3 id=mirror-block>
Mirror block</h3>

A **mirror block** holds a verbatim copy of another block, called its <dfn for=mirror>target block</dfn>,
so that the target block can be restored when it is damaged.
Mirror blocks are only defined for block types whose damage affects more than a single [=parcel=]
and can be detected by reading the block alone, i.e. that have a checksum:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>Target Block Types
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>C10<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C17<sub>16</sub>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;17<sub>16</sub> ([[#metadata-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C50<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C57<sub>16</sub>
      <td>50<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;57<sub>16</sub> ([[#dictionary-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C58<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C5F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>58<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;5F<sub>16</sub> ([[#import-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C60<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C63<sub>16</sub>
      <td>60<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;63<sub>16</sub> ([[#module-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C64<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C67<sub>16</sub>
      <td>64<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;67<sub>16</sub> ([[#resource-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C70<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;C77<sub>16</sub>
      <td>70<sub>16</sub>&nbsp;—&nbsp;77<sub>16</sub> ([[#formula-block]])
  </tbody>
</table>

As with any other block, the [=block/size=] field of a mirror block is determined by its own [=block/type=]
and counts the bytes of its own [=block/data=].
Since a mirror block is longer than its target block, its [=block/size=] field may have to be wider.
Therefore a mirror block is either:

* <dfn for=mirror>Unwidened</dfn>, where the [=block/size=] fields of the mirror block and its target block have the same width,
    and its [=mirror/target type=] is its [=block/type=] minus C00<sub>16</sub>; or
* <dfn for=mirror>Widened</dfn>, where the [=block/size=] field of the mirror block is one step wider than that of its target block,
    and its [=mirror/target type=] is its [=block/type=] minus C01<sub>16</sub>.
    Its [=block/type=] % 4 MUST NOT be 0.

The [=mirror/target block=] MUST be of the <dfn for=mirror>target type</dfn>,
which MUST be a block type listed above for the mirror block's [=block/type=].
Whether a mirror block is widened is signalled by [=mirror/target_offset=].

<div class=note>
  For example, a mirror block of type C13<sub>16</sub> mirrors a metadata block of type 13<sub>16</sub>,
  which is uncompressed and has a [=u64le=] [=block/size=] field, if unwidened,
  or a metadata block of type 12<sub>16</sub> with a [=u32le=] [=block/size=] field if widened.
  Since C00<sub>16</sub> % 4 = 0, both stay in the same row of the table above.
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=mirror>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>mirror_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][8] ∈ {[=const/MIRROR_MAGIC=], [=const/OVERLAY_MIRROR_MAGIC=]}
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=block/block_start=] - [=archive/archive_start=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>target_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ 0
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>target_data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>mirror_checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u64le=]
  </tbody>
</table>

[=mirror/mirror_magic=] is a fixed byte sequence that allows mirror blocks to be found by scanning
when the surrounding structure, such as the header, the footer or the metadata chain, is damaged.
It MUST be [=const/OVERLAY_MIRROR_MAGIC=] in an [=overlay archive=] and [=const/MIRROR_MAGIC=] otherwise.

[=mirror/archive_offset=] is the offset from [=archive/archive_start=] to the [=block/block_start=] of the mirror block.

<div class=note>
  [=mirror/archive_offset=] distinguishes mirror blocks of this archive from those of another archive stored inside it,
  which can be found by scanning and verified by themselves just like the mirror blocks of this archive.
  Such a mirror block may point to bytes that are not its target block in this archive,
  for example when the inner archive is split into multiple blocks.
  Since the inner archive is stored after [=archive/archive_start=] of this archive,
  the start of the archive implied by its [=mirror/archive_offset=] is always greater.
</div>

[=mirror/target_offset=] is the offset from the [=block/block_start=] of the [=mirror/target block=]
to the [=block/block_start=] of the mirror block, plus 1 if the mirror block is [=mirror/widened=].
Since the offset between any two blocks is even, [=mirror/target_offset=] % 2 tells whether the mirror block is widened.
The target block always precedes the mirror block.

[=mirror/target_data=] is a copy of the [=block/data=] of the target block.
The bytes of the target block, from its [=block/block_start=] to its [=block/block_end=],
MUST be equal to the concatenation of the following:

1. The shortest [=leb128=] encoding of the [=mirror/target type=];
2. The length of [=mirror/target_data=], encoded as the [=block/size=] field for the [=mirror/target type=];
3. [=mirror/target_data=].

The [=block/padding=] of the target block, if any, is not compared, and is restored as a zero byte.
The [=block/type=] of the mirror block itself is 2 bytes long, as every block type uses the shortest [=leb128=] encoding.

[=mirror/mirror_checksum=] is the XXH64 [[!XXHASH]] hash with the seed zero
of all bytes from the [=block/block_start=] of the mirror block to [=mirror/checksum_end=],
including its [=block/type=] and [=block/size=] fields.

</div>

<div class=note>
  Unlike other checksums in this specification, [=mirror/mirror_checksum=] is computed over the stored bytes,
  so that the copy can be verified without decoding it and the mirror block can be verified when found by scanning.
  A decompressor normally uses a mirror block only after the checksum of its target block fails,
  while a verifier can also compare the copy with the target block directly.
</div>

<div class=note>
  A mirror block is a copy of bytes, not a block to be interpreted at its own position.
  This is necessary because relative offsets in a metadata block are measured from the metadata block itself.
</div>

A mirror block SHOULD be [=mirror/unwidened=] unless its [=block/data=] doesn't fit in the [=block/size=] field for the target type.

<div class=note>
  The data of a mirror block is at least 18 bytes longer than that of its target block,
  so this is only relevant when the target block is close to the limit of its [=block/size=] field.
  A single step is always sufficient, since the next width can hold far more than the additional bytes.
  The width of the target block's [=block/size=] field can't be inferred from the size of [=mirror/target_data=],
  because a block may use a wider [=block/size=] field than necessary.
</div>

<div class=note>
  A block with a [=u64le=] [=block/size=] field can't be mirrored when its data is too close to 2<sup>64</sup> bytes,
  since its mirror block can't be widened.
  This is not a practical concern, because such a block is far larger than 2<sup>63</sup> bytes,
  which is beyond what most systems with signed 64-bit file offsets can store in a single file.
</div>

<div class=note>
  To find mirror blocks by scanning, a decoder can look for [=const/MIRROR_MAGIC=]
  and try each of four possible widths of the [=block/size=] field preceding it.
  The candidate is correct when the 2 bytes before the [=block/size=] field encode a mirror block type with the matching width
  and [=mirror/mirror_checksum=] verifies.
  The candidate is then discarded unless its [=block/block_start=] minus [=mirror/archive_offset=]
  equals [=archive/archive_start=], because it belongs to another archive stored inside this archive otherwise.
  If [=archive/archive_start=] can't be determined from [=archive/header_magic=] or [=archive/size_hint=],
  the smallest value implied by verified candidates can be used instead.
  A decoder that doesn't know whether the archive is an [=overlay archive=], e.g. because both the header and the footer are damaged,
  looks for both magics, and the magic of verified candidates tells it.
</div>

<div class=note>
  Mirror blocks have a distinct magic in an overlay archive for the same reason as the header and the footer ([[#unencrypted-archive]]):
  every structure that can be found without the header has to tell whether the archive is an overlay archive.
</div>

A mirror block doesn't belong to any [=segment=] and MAY occur anywhere in [=archive/blocks=],
including after the last metadata block, subject only to the requirement that its target block precedes it.
A single target block MAY have multiple mirror blocks.
The requirements on a mirror block in this section are requirements on conforming compressors,
and a mirror block violating them doesn't make the archive an [=invalid archive=].
Mirror blocks don't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=] at all.

A mirror block is <dfn for=mirror>intact</dfn> if its [=mirror/mirror_checksum=] verifies
and it satisfies the requirements above other than being equal to its target block.
A decompressor MUST NOT restore a target block from a mirror block that is not [=mirror/intact=],
and SHOULD report a mirror block that is not intact or differs from its intact target block when it reads one.

<div class=note>
  A mirror block is as likely to be damaged as any other block,
  but its damage loses nothing as long as its target block is intact.
  Treating it as an invalid archive would reject an archive whose files are all readable,
  and only by decompressors that happen to read mirror blocks.
</div>

A mirror block and its target block are expected to be damaged together when they are close to each other.
Conforming compressors SHOULD separate the end of the target block and the [=block/block_start=] of its mirror block
by at least 4096 bytes, so that they never share an aligned 4 KiB unit of the underlying storage,
and SHOULD separate them by at least 4 MiB (4,194,304 bytes) when possible.

Issue: The recommended distances are tentative, pending the measurement of burst lengths of latent sector errors
(`docs/recovery-design.md` §8, R1).
The interval between mirrored headers in an encrypted archive ([[#encrypted-archive]]) changes with the latter distance.

A damaged encrypted chunk fails to authenticate as a whole,
and an aligned 4 KiB unit of the underlying storage may span two adjacent encrypted chunks.
Therefore, in place of the distance of 4096 bytes recommended above,
conforming compressors SHOULD place a mirror block and its target block in an encrypted archive
so that at least one entire encrypted chunk lies between them.

<div class=note>
  Dictionary, module and formula blocks typically occur early in their segments,
  so their mirror blocks can be placed near the end of the segment.
  A metadata block, on the other hand, only becomes available at the end of its segment,
  so its mirror block can be placed after a sufficient amount of the next segment has been written.
  The mirror block of the last metadata block can't be distant from it unless the compressor delays the last segment,
  and closing segments periodically bounds the loss in that case.
</div>


<h3 id=repair>
Repair</h3>

<div class=note>
  Restoring damaged blocks from mirror blocks is a process separate from [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=]:
  it takes a damaged byte sequence and replaces each target block whose bytes differ from an [=mirror/intact=] mirror block
  with the bytes reconstructed from the mirror block,
  locating the target block with [=mirror/target_offset=] alone.
</div>

Issue: The repair process is to be specified in this section (`docs/recovery-design.md` §5.6).

Issue: Repairing an encrypted archive with mirror blocks or mirrored headers requires decrypting
the encrypted chunks after one that fails to authenticate, which a standard age implementation doesn't do.
This is to be specified with the repair process as a separate step producing the decrypted bytes,
where bytes of such chunks are marked as erasures and the interpretation of an archive stays unchanged
(`docs/recovery-design.md` §5.6).

<div class=note>
  A repaired encrypted chunk can't be encrypted again with the original file key and [=envelope/payload_nonce=]
  unless its plaintext is fully restored, because doing so otherwise reuses a nonce for a different plaintext.
  Since a damaged chunk usually contains [=leaf blocks=] that mirror blocks don't cover,
  a repaired encrypted archive is expected to be decrypted, or encrypted again with a new file key.
</div>

<h2 id=optional-metadata>
Optional metadata</h2>

The [=meta/optional_metadata=] field of the metadata block ([[#metadata-block]]) holds the **optional metadata**, encoded in CBOR [[!RFC8949]].
This part encodes any metadata that is not strictly necessary to read the file,
including associated dates and times, access control information and comments.

The optional metadata is described by the following CDDL [[RFC8610]] definitions:

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-opt-metadata = {
  aqr-archive-comment
  ? &(entry-extras: -2) => [* aqr-entry-extra / null]  ; Indexed by entry numbers, starting from the root directory

    * $$aqr-opt-metadata-member  ; For future extensions
}

$$aqr-opt-metadata-member = ()  ; No extension is defined by this specification

aqr-entry-extra = {
  aqr-entry-timestamps
  aqr-entry-comment
  aqr-entry-link-group
  aqr-entry-windows
  aqr-entry-unix
  aqr-entry-zip

    * $$aqr-entry-extra-member  ; For future extensions
}

$$aqr-entry-extra-member = ()  ; No extension is defined by this specification

; TODO: other extras for round trip
</pre>

It MUST be a single well-formed CBOR data item [[!RFC8949]] without any following bytes,
and no map in it can have duplicate keys.
The value of every key defined by this specification MUST match its definition.
If [=meta/optional_metadata_size=] is 0, the optional metadata is an empty map `{}`.

The members of `aqr-entry-extra` are grouped by their purposes, and each group is described in the following sections.
Keys of `aqr-entry-extra` from -1 to -59 are platform-neutral.
Keys from -60 to -69 are specific to Windows, -70 to -79 to Unix, -80 to -89 to macOS, and -90 to -99 to other platforms.
Keys from -100 are specific to the formats an entry was converted from.

<h3 id=timestamps>Timestamps</h3>

The keys `crtime`, `ctime`, `mtime`, `atime` and `arctime` of `aqr-entry-extra`
record the timestamps that file systems and archive formats associate with a file, a directory or a link.
Each is an `aqr-time` described below, and is absent if the source doesn't have the corresponding timestamp.

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-timestamps = (
  ? &(crtime: -1) => aqr-time
  ? &(ctime: -2) => aqr-time
  ? &(mtime: -3) => aqr-time
  ? &(atime: -4) => aqr-time
  ? &(arctime: -5) => aqr-time
)
</pre>

: `crtime`
:: The creation time, also known as the birth time: when the file was created at its location.
    It is not updated afterwards, but copying a file usually creates a new one with the time of copying.
    Examples include the creation time of NTFS and FAT, the create date of HFS+ and APFS,
    `i_crtime` of ext4 and `otime` of Btrfs,
    which are exposed as `st_birthtime` on BSD and macOS and as `stx_btime` of `statx` on Linux.
: `ctime`
:: The status change time:
    when the file's metadata (e.g. permissions, ownership or link count) or its content was last changed.
    It is the POSIX `st_ctime`, and the change time of NTFS and the attribute modification date of HFS+ are similar.
    POSIX doesn't allow it to be set to an arbitrary time,
    so an extracting decompressor usually can't restore it.
: `mtime`
:: The modification time: when the file's content was last changed.
    For a directory, it is when an entry was last added, removed or renamed in it.
    This is the timestamp that virtually all file systems and archive formats have.
: `atime`
:: The access time: when the file's content was last read.
    Many systems update it lazily or not at all for performance
    (e.g. `relatime` and `noatime` on Linux, and the access time updates of NTFS that can be disabled),
    and FAT only keeps the date.
    Reading a file for archiving can itself update it.
: `arctime`
:: The backup time: when the file was last backed up, as recorded by backup software.
    File systems don't update it by themselves.
    An example is the backup date of HFS and HFS+, exposed as `ATTR_CMN_BKUPTIME` of `getattrlist` on macOS.
    It is the time stored by the source, not the time when the archive itself was made.

<div class=note>
  The name "ctime" is ambiguous.
  Windows and formats originating from it use "C" for the creation time:
  `st_ctime` in the Microsoft C runtime, the `Ctime` of ZIP extra field 0x000a,
  and the `CTime` of 7z and RAR all go to `crtime`, not `ctime`.
  POSIX formats such as pax use it for the status change time, which goes to `ctime`.
</div>

`aqr-time` represents a point in time or a local date and time,
together with a [=quantization interval=] and optional time zone information.
It is described by the following CDDL definitions,
where `etime` is the CDDL type name for the extended time defined in [[!RFC9581]]:

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-time =
  $aqr-base-time /                          ; UTC timestamp, scaled and quantized
  [$aqr-base-time] /                        ; Local time in unknown time zone, encoded like UTC (*)
  [$aqr-instant, offset: aqr-tz-offset] /   ; UTC timestamp with time zone offset ($)
  etime /                                   ; Extended time defined in [[!RFC9581]]
  [etime]                                   ; Local time in unknown time zone, encoded like UTC

aqr-tz-offset = -48..56  ; In 15 minutes, positive east of UTC as in ISO 9660

$aqr-base-time  = #6.100(int)    ; 1d (FAT atime*), date as in [[!RFC8943]]
$aqr-base-time /= $aqr-instant  ; Can be given a time zone offset

$aqr-instant  = #6.121(int)  ; 2s (FAT mtime*, exFAT atime$, ZIP*, CAB*)
$aqr-instant /= int          ; 1s (~ext3, HFS/HFS+, tar, ISO 9660$)
$aqr-instant /= #6.122(int)  ; 10ms (FAT ctime*, ISO 9660$, exFAT$)
$aqr-instant /= #6.123(int)  ; 1ms
$aqr-instant /= #6.124(int)  ; 1us (UDF)
$aqr-instant /= #6.125(int)  ; 100ns (NTFS, RAR*, 7z)
$aqr-instant /= #6.126(int)  ; 1ns (ext4, ZFS, Btrfs, APFS)
</pre>

A base time value `$aqr-base-time` is an integer, either untagged or tagged as follows.
The integer multiplied by the scale is the number of seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z,
not counting leap seconds as in the CBOR tag 1 [[!RFC8949]];
negative values denote the time before that epoch.
The tag also determines the quantization interval,
which is relative to the time value and specified in [[#etime-quantization-interval]].
`#6.100` is the number of days since 1970-01-01 [[!RFC8943]],
and is the only base time value that is not an `$aqr-instant`, i.e. it can't be given a time zone offset.
<table class=data>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>Encoding</th>
  <th>Quantization Interval (<abbr title="seconds">s</abbr>)</th>
  <th>Scale (<abbr title="seconds">s</abbr>)</th>
  <th>Examples</th>
</tr>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.100(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 86400]</td>
  <td>86400</td>
  <td>FAT atime*</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.121(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 2]</td>
  <td>2</td>
  <td>FAT mtime*, exFAT atime$, ZIP*, CAB*</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>int</code></td>
  <td>[0, 1]</td>
  <td>1</td>
  <td>ext3 and earlier, HFS/HFS+, tar, ISO 9660$ (directory records)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.122(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-2</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-2</sup></td>
  <td>FAT ctime*, ISO 9660$ (volume descriptors), exFAT$</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.123(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-3</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-3</sup></td>
  <td>-</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.124(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-6</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-6</sup></td>
  <td>UDF</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.125(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-7</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-7</sup></td>
  <td>NTFS, RAR*, 7z</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.126(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-9</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-9</sup></td>
  <td>ext4, ZFS, Btrfs, APFS</td>
</tr>
</table>

In the Examples column, formats marked with * store (or can store) a local time in an unknown time zone,
and formats marked with $ store a local time with its offset from UTC.

<div class=note>
  Tags 121 to 126 are the tags for enumerated alternatives 0 to 5 [[CBOR-NOTABLE-TAGS]],
  whose meanings are given by the schema in use rather than by the tags themselves.
  They are therefore meaningful only as `$aqr-instant`,
  and are not shortcuts for the extended time in general.
</div>

<div class=note>
  Since a time zone offset is a multiple of 15 minutes, converting a local time to UTC keeps it a multiple of 2 seconds.
  A local date, however, doesn't stay a multiple of 1 day unless the offset is zero,
  so a date with a known time zone offset can only be given as an extended time
  (e.g. `1001({1: 1697641200, -14: 86400, -10: "+09:00"})` for 2023-10-19 in UTC+9).
</div>

Each alternative of `aqr-time` has the following meaning:

: `$aqr-base-time`
:: A point in time without any time zone information.
: `[$aqr-base-time]`
:: A local date and time in an unknown time zone, encoded as if it were UTC.
    It does not identify a single point in time.
: `[$aqr-instant, aqr-tz-offset]`
:: A point in time, as in the bare `$aqr-base-time`,
    with the offset of the time zone where it was recorded, in 15-minute increments as in ISO 9660.
    The offset is positive east of UTC: UTC+<var>x</var> hours is 4<var>x</var> and UTC&minus;<var>x</var> hours is &minus;4<var>x</var>.
    The local time is the base time value plus 900 times the offset in seconds.
: `etime`
:: An extended time [[RFC9581]] (tag 1001), for any value which doesn't fit the other alternatives.
    The time zone information, if any, is given as a Time Zone Hint (key -10 or 10),
    and the quantization interval is given as a key described in [[#etime-quantization-interval]].
: `[etime]`
:: A local date and time in an unknown time zone, encoded as if it were UTC.
    The extended time MUST NOT contain a Time Zone Hint (key -10 or 10),
    and its timescale (key -1, -13 or 13), if present, MUST be 0 (UTC).

`etime` or `[etime]` MUST NOT be used
if another alternative of `aqr-time` represents the same time value,
the same quantization interval and the same time zone information.
A Time Zone Hint is the same as an `aqr-tz-offset`
only when it is a numeric offset (e.g. `"+09:00"`) equal to that offset;
an extended time without any quantization interval is never the same as other alternatives.

<div class=note>
  As a consequence, timestamps from common file systems and archive formats never need the extended time,
  and an implementation without full support for [[RFC9581]] can still handle them.
  Such an implementation can ignore an `aqr-time` using the extended time,
  as an extracting decompressor is allowed to ignore any extra metadata ([[#extraction]]).
</div>

<div class=note>
  Some precisions found in the wild are only representable with the extended time,
  e.g. minutes in ASN.1 `UTCTime`, 2<sup>-32</sup> s in NTP and 1/300 s in SQL Server `DATETIME`,
  and so are time zone offsets that are not multiples of 15 minutes as in UDF.
  They are rare enough in file metadata not to deserve their own encodings.
</div>

Two `aqr-time` values in different encodings are different even when they denote the same point in time.
For example, `1` and `122(100)` differ in their quantization intervals.
A compressor converting a timestamp from a file system or another archive format
SHOULD preserve its precision, i.e. use an encoding whose quantization interval matches the source.

<div class=note>
  Comparing timestamps from file systems of different precisions is a common source of problems,
  e.g. an unchanged file copied from ext4 to FAT appears to be modified.
  Recording the quantization interval for each timestamp, rather than for each archive,
  lets any consumer tell whether two timestamps can denote the same time,
  even after an archive mixes multiple sources by editing.
</div>

<div class=example>
  The following are possible encodings of 2023-10-19T14:12:34.87Z, or 23:12:34.87 in UTC+9,
  depending on the source:

    * ZIP with a DOS date and time, a local time in an unknown time zone: `[121(848878577)]`
    * ZIP with an extended timestamp extra field (0x5455) in UTC: `1697724754`
    * git, which records a time zone offset in minutes: `[1697724754, 36]`
    * exFAT modification time with a UTC offset: `[122(169772475487), 36]`
    * NTFS: `125(16977247548700000)`
    * ext4: `126(1697724754870000000)`
    * FAT access date, a local date in an unknown time zone: `[100(19649)]`
    * A source rounding to the nearest second: `1001({1: 1697724755, -14: [0.5, 0.5]})`
</div>

Timestamps from ZIP are discussed in [[#timestamps-from-zip]].

<h3 id=comments>Comments</h3>

`archive-comment` of `aqr-opt-metadata` and `comment` of `aqr-entry-extra`
are free-form comments attached to the whole archive and to each entry respectively,
e.g. an archive comment and a file comment of ZIP or RAR.
Both are an `aqr-text`, which is a Unicode string,
a WTF-8 [[!WTF-8]] string with lone surrogates, or a byte sequence in an unknown encoding:

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-archive-comment = (
  ? &(archive-comment: -1) => aqr-text
)

aqr-entry-comment = (
  ? &(comment: -6) => aqr-text
)

aqr-text =
  tstr /          ; Unicode
  #6.273(bstr) /  ; WTF-8, i.e. UTF-16 + surrogate compat
  bstr            ; In unknown encoding
</pre>

An `aqr-text` MUST be a `tstr` if it can be represented as one,
i.e. if it is a sequence of Unicode scalar values.
The byte string in `#6.273(bstr)` MUST be a valid WTF-8 string that is not a valid UTF-8 string,
i.e. it MUST contain at least one encoded lone surrogate.
A `bstr` is not subject to these requirements even if it happens to be a valid UTF-8 string,
as its encoding is not known.

Comments from ZIP are discussed in [[#comments-from-zip]].

<h3 id=link-groups><code>link-group</code>: Hard links</h3>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-link-group = (
  ? &(link-group: -7) => int  ; File nodes only
)
</pre>

`link-group` records that [=file nodes=] were originally the same file,
i.e. hard links to the same inode on Unix or to the same file record on NTFS.
It has no effect on other nodes, and a compressor SHOULD NOT give it to them.
A non-negative value is <dfn>segment-local</dfn> and denotes the same group only within the same [=segment=],
while a negative value is <dfn>archive-wide</dfn> and denotes the same group in every segment.
The resulting [=file node/link group=] in the [=archive value=] is determined by [=interpret a segment|interpreting a segment=].

A compressor MUST NOT give the same [=file node/link group=] to files that were not the same file.
It SHOULD NOT give a [=file node/link group=] that no other file node has.
A file node with `link-group` SHOULD refer to the same [=leaf block=] as other file nodes in the same group,
and SHOULD have the same [=entry extra=] as them except for `link-group` and `comment`.

<div class=note>
  Segment-local groups allow the inode number (`st_ino`) to be used as is,
  as CBOR can encode any 64-bit unsigned integer as a non-negative `int`,
  and can be assigned without reading earlier segments.
  Inode numbers are still only unique within a single file system,
  so a compressor reading from multiple file systems has to renumber some of them.
  Archive-wide groups link files across segments,
  e.g. when a compressor periodically starts a new segment while archiving a single tree,
  or when it appends a segment to an existing archive.
  A compressor using them is responsible for keeping them consistent with all other segments,
  e.g. by reading earlier metadata blocks when appending.
  Inode numbers are not suitable as archive-wide values as is,
  because they can be reused after the file is removed or come from an unrelated file system,
  and linking unrelated files with the same content can't be detected by any decompressor.
</div>

A [=file node/link group=] is <dfn>consistent</dfn> if all file nodes with that group
in the final [=archive value=] have the same [=file node/content=].
An inconsistent group, e.g. after a later segment has replaced only some of its file nodes,
is not an [=invalid archive=] failure, but has no effect on extraction ([[#extraction]]).

<div class=note>
  The consistency can only be determined from the final archive value,
  because a later segment may replace any file node in the group.
  Treating an inconsistent group as an invalid archive would make a decompressor reading only some segments
  unable to detect such failure ([[#partial-access]]).
  A decompressor can instead check the consistency before creating hard links,
  at no cost when file nodes refer to the same leaf block.
  Hard links are not part of the archive model, so a decompressor ignoring `link-group`
  still extracts the correct files with the same contents.
  Directory hard links, found in some macOS Time Machine volumes, are not represented.
</div>

<h3 id=windows-extras>Windows-specific metadata</h3>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-windows = (
  ? &(win-link-type: -60) => &(file: 0, directory: 1, junction: 2)  ; Link nodes only

  ; TODO: file attributes (e.g. ZIP external file attributes)
)
</pre>

`win-link-type` determines the kind of a link to be created on Windows ([[#extraction]]).
It has no effect on nodes other than [=link nodes=], and a compressor SHOULD NOT give it to them.
"`file`" and "`directory`" denote a symbolic link to a file and to a directory respectively,
and "`junction`" denotes a junction (a mount point reparse point).

<h3 id=unix-extras>Unix-specific metadata</h3>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-unix = (
  ? &(unix-mode: -70) => uint .le 4095  ; Permission bits only (0o7777), without S_IFMT
  ? &(unix-uid: -71) => uint
  ? &(unix-gid: -72) => uint
)
</pre>

`unix-mode` only holds the permission bits, including the set-user-ID, set-group-ID and sticky bits.
The kind of an entry is solely determined by its node ([[#archive-model]]), so `unix-mode` never includes the file type bits (`S_IFMT`).

`unix-uid` and `unix-gid` are the numeric user and group IDs of the owner.

Unix metadata from ZIP is discussed in [[#unix-extras-from-zip]].

<h3 id=zip-extras><code>zip-extras</code>: ZIP extra fields</h3>

`zip-extras` preserves extra fields [[APPNOTE]] of the ZIP entry that the entry was converted from,
when they carry information about the file that has no other representation in this specification.

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-zip = (
  ? &(zip-extras: -100) => aqr-zip-extras
)

aqr-zip-extras = { * aqr-zip-extra-key => bstr .size (0..65531) }
aqr-zip-extra-key =
  0..65535 /   ; Header ID n in the local header
  -65536..-1   ; Header ID n in the central header, as -1 - n
; TODO: multiple extra fields with the same header ID
</pre>

A key |n| from 0 to 65535 denotes the extra field with the header ID |n| in the local header,
and a key &minus;1 &minus; |n| from &minus;65536 to &minus;1 denotes the extra field with the header ID |n| in the central header.
The value is the data of the extra field, excluding its 4-byte header of the header ID and the data size.
Therefore a value with a non-negative key is always in the format for the local header,
and a value with a negative key is always in the format for the central header.

A compressor converting a ZIP archive MAY put any extra field of an entry into `zip-extras`, subject to the following requirements:

* It MUST NOT put the central header version of an extra field
    if the local header version with the same header ID is identical byte by byte.
    The central header version without the local header version is only put with a negative key.
* It MUST NOT put an extra field whose information is fully represented elsewhere,
    i.e. in the entry name, the archive model ([[#archive-model]]) or any other key of `aqr-entry-extra`.
* It MUST NOT put an extra field which is only meaningful to the ZIP container itself,
    e.g. sizes, offsets, padding, alignment, encryption and signatures over the ZIP archive.
* It SHOULD NOT give `zip-extras` to the root directory, which has no counterpart in ZIP.

Extra fields known to be subject to these requirements are listed in [[#zip-extras-to-remove]].
Any other extra field, including one with an unregistered header ID, can be put into `zip-extras` as is.

`zip-extras` is never authoritative.
If information in a preserved extra field disagrees with the archive model or any other key of `aqr-entry-extra`,
the latter MUST be used.
A decompressor finding an extra field that a compressor is not allowed to put SHOULD ignore that field only,
and SHOULD NOT treat the whole archive as invalid.

<div class=note>
  `zip-extras` is designed for a one-way conversion from ZIP;
  reconstructing the original ZIP archive from the converted archive is not a goal.
  Extra fields are preserved so that information without its own key yet,
  e.g. platform-specific attributes, access control lists and resource forks,
  can be recovered from already converted archives once a dedicated key is defined.
  Unknown extra fields are preserved for the same reason,
  even though some of them might be only meaningful to the ZIP container.
  The local and central header versions of the same header ID often differ in their formats,
  e.g. the central header version of 0x5455 only has the modification time,
  and that of many Info-ZIP attribute blocks only has a summary.
  Distinguishing them by keys lets a consumer interpret each value in the correct format.
  The key for the central header version is &minus;1 &minus; |n| instead of &minus;|n|,
  so that header ID 0 doesn't collide with the local header version,
  and both versions have the same encoded length in CBOR.
</div>

<h2 id=extraction>
Extraction</h2>

Writing an [=archive value=] to a file system is outside the archive model.
A decompressor doing so is subject to the following requirements:

* It MUST NOT create, modify or remove any file outside the destination directory,
    including through symbolic links or other redirections already present in the destination
    or created during the extraction.
* It SHOULD map an [=entry name=] whose [=entry name/encoding=] is "`wtf-8`" to the platform's native Unicode name
    (e.g. UTF-16 with lone surrogates on Windows), and
    an [=entry name=] whose [=entry name/encoding=] is "`raw`" to the identical byte sequence where the platform allows.
* If an [=entry name=] can't be represented on the platform or has a special meaning there
    (e.g. a name containing `\` or `:`, or a reserved device name on Windows),
    it MUST either fail or report a substituted name to the user; it MUST NOT silently use a different name.
* If multiple [=entry names=] in a directory map to the same file system name
    (e.g. due to case insensitivity or Unicode normalization),
    it MUST either fail or report the conflict, and MUST NOT write a later entry through an earlier one.
* It MAY ignore the extra of any node, e.g. [=file node/extra=] and [=directory node/extra=].
    It SHOULD NOT apply ownership information such as `unix-uid` and `unix-gid` unless explicitly requested.

<div class=note>
  The archive model can't express a path through a [=link node=] ([[#archive-model]]),
  but a link created during the extraction can still redirect a later write,
  e.g. when a link `A` and a file `a/x` map to the same directory on a case-insensitive file system.
  The rule on conflicting names above already prevents this, and the first requirement makes it explicit.
  Possible implementations include creating all links after all other nodes,
  or opening each path relative to its parent directory without following links
  (e.g. `openat` with `O_NOFOLLOW`, or `RESOLVE_BENEATH` on Linux).
</div>

A decompressor extracting a [=link node=] is subject to the following additional requirements:

* It MUST NOT create a link whose target escapes the destination directory unless explicitly requested,
    and SHOULD provide a way to request it.
    A [=link target=] escapes the destination directory
    if it is absolute or relative to a drive or a root on the platform,
    or if resolving it from the parent directory of the link,
    following any [=link nodes=] in the [=archive value=] on the way,
    reaches a parent of the destination directory.
    A link not created for this reason MUST be reported to the user.

    <div class=note>
      Creating such a link doesn't write outside the destination directory by itself,
      but the extracted tree can lead later users or programs to do so.
      Resolving a target lexically is not enough:
      when `d/e/l` is a link to `../../q`, a link to `d/e/l/../..` lexically resolves to `d`
      but actually reaches the parent of the destination directory.
    </div>
* On Windows, it SHOULD replace every `/` in the [=link target=] with `\`.
    It SHOULD create the kind of link given by `win-link-type`, if any.
    Otherwise, it SHOULD create a symbolic link to a directory if the target resolves to a [=directory node=]
    in the [=archive value=] as above, and a symbolic link to a file otherwise.
* If a link can't be created, e.g. due to the lack of privilege, the file system or the length of its target,
    it MUST either fail or report it to the user; it MUST NOT silently create a different kind of node.
    It MAY create a copy of the target instead if it reports so.
* It MUST NOT apply the [=link node/extra=] of a link node to its target.
    Timestamps, ownership and permissions SHOULD be applied to the link itself where the platform allows (e.g. `lutimes`, `lchown`).

A decompressor MAY create hard links among [=file nodes=] with the same [=consistent=] [=file node/link group=].
It MUST NOT create hard links among any other file nodes, even when they have the same content.

<div class=note>
  Hard links let extracted files share the disk space, and changes made to one of them,
  which is crucial for e.g. snapshot backups made with `rsync --link-dest`.
  But files that were independent have to remain independent.
</div>

A decompressor SHOULD NOT create a FIFO or a device file for a [=special node=] unless explicitly requested,
as with ownership information.
A special node not created for this reason, or because the platform can't represent it
(e.g. the lack of privilege, or a device number out of the platform's range),
MUST be reported to the user.

<div class=note>
  Creating a device file usually needs the superuser privilege,
  and a device file with lax permissions created by such an extraction can expose the entire disk.
  FIFOs are harmless by themselves, but are treated the same for simplicity.
</div>

A decompressor MUST NOT create anything for an [=unknown node=], and MUST report it to the user.
It MAY continue extracting other nodes.

<h2 id=conformance>
Conformance</h2>

As well as sections marked as non-normative, all diagrams, examples and notes in this specification are non-normative.
Everything else in this specification is normative.

The key words MAY, MUST, MUST NOT, RECOMMENDED, SHOULD and SHOULD NOT in this specification
are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[!RFC2119]] [[!RFC8174]]
when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.

<h3 id=conformance-levels>
Conformance levels</h3>

A conforming decompressor has a <dfn>conformance level</dfn>,
one of the following according to the modules ([[#modules]]) it applies:

: <dfn>Level 0</dfn>
:: Applies no module.
: <dfn>Level 1</dfn>
:: Applies every [=registered module=], i.e. every module whose [=module hash=] is the [=registered hash=]
    of an entry of the Aqueous Modules Registry ([[#modules-registry]]), and no other module.
: <dfn>Level 2</dfn>
:: Applies every module.

A decompressor at [=level 0=] or [=level 1=] MAY refuse to apply a module that its level doesn't include,
in which case a [=resource failure=] occurs.
A decompressor at [=level 2=] MUST NOT refuse to apply a module,
though it may still fail with a [=resource failure=] when a module exceeds its limits.
A level doesn't change any other requirement on a decompressor,
e.g. a decompressor at [=level 0=] still reads formula blocks without [[#insn-apply|Apply instructions]].

<div class=note>
  A decompressor at [=level 0=] or [=level 1=] never runs untrusted code.
  A module registered after a decompressor at [=level 1=] was made is not known to it,
  which results in an [=unsupported failure=] if the module is given by a [=shorthand block=]
  and in a [=resource failure=] otherwise, until the decompressor is updated.
  See [[#module-block]] for how each level can apply its modules.
</div>

<h3 id=partial-access>
Partial access</h3>

A decompressor MAY compute only a part of the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=],
such as a subset of entries, a byte range of a [=file node/content=], or entry names without contents.
Such a partial result MUST be equal to the corresponding part of the full result, if the full result is an [=archive value=].

A decompressor computing a partial result is not required to detect an [=invalid archive=] failure
caused only by parts it doesn't compute.
It SHOULD verify every checksum covering only the bytes it has computed.

<div class=note>
  For example, a decompressor reading a byte range of a file defined by a formula block
  only reads the non-leaf blocks covering that range, rather than its entire [=parcel=].
  It can't verify the output checksum of the formula block,
  but can verify the checksums of non-leaf blocks it has read.
  A decompressor looking up a single path has to consider every [=segment=] that may replace it,
  which is possible by reading metadata blocks backward from [=archive/metadata_backward_offset=]
  until the path or one of its ancestors is found as a non-directory entry in a segment.
  If that entry is the path itself and not a [=tombstone=], it gives the node at the path;
  otherwise, i.e. if it is a tombstone or an ancestor, the path doesn't exist.
  If no segment has such an entry, the path doesn't exist in a [=standalone archive=],
  and is looked up in the [=base=] of an [=overlay archive=].
  Likewise, reading a file using [=import/import|imports=] only needs the imported byte ranges,
  and an [=internal import=] is resolved by the same lookup from the preceding segment.
</div>

<h2 id=security-considerations>
Security considerations</h2>

An archive is a container for arbitrary data and doesn't by itself contain active content,
except for WebAssembly modules ([[#module-execution]]) that a decompressor executes to reconstruct file contents.
Such modules are run under a deterministic profile without access to anything but their inputs and outputs,
but a decompressor still has to treat them as untrusted code and isolate them accordingly,
unless it knows their [=module hash=] ([[#module-block]]).
Formula blocks and modules can produce outputs far larger than the archive itself,
and some fields (e.g. [=envelope/lg_work_factor=]) directly control the amount of computation.
A decompressor is expected to enforce implementation-defined limits and fail with a [=resource failure=] when they are exceeded.
A decompressor writing an archive to a file system is subject to the requirements in [[#extraction]],
in particular to prevent writing outside the destination directory.
An unencrypted archive provides no confidentiality,
and its XXH64 checksums ([[#checksums]]) only detect accidental corruption, not intentional modification.
An encrypted archive ([[#encrypted-archive]]) provides confidentiality and integrity through [[AGE]],
which is only as strong as the passphrase used.
The metadata of an archive, including entry names, timestamps and ownership,
may reveal information about the system that created it.

An overlay archive is interpreted against a base, typically a directory on the file system,
and can copy any part of the base into its result.
A decompressor reading a base is subject to the requirements in [[#bases]],
in particular to use a base only when explicitly given one and to never read outside it.
Applying an overlay archive in place modifies and removes files in the base directory.

<h2 id=iana-considerations>
IANA Considerations</h2>

<h3 id=media-type>
<code>application/aqr</code> Media Type</h3>

<p>This document requests the registration of a new media type in the Media Types Registry ([[!RFC6838]], section 5.6) as follows:</p>

<dl>
<dt>Type name</dt>
<dd>application</dd>
<dt>Subtype name</dt>
<dd>aqr</dd>
<dt>Required parameters</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Optional parameters</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Encoding considerations</dt>
<dd>binary</dd>
<dt>Security considerations</dt>
<dd>See [[#security-considerations]].</dd>
<dt>Interoperability considerations</dt>
<dd>
  <p>An unencrypted archive may be preceded and followed by arbitrary bytes, e.g. a self-extracting executable stub;
  its extent is determined by [=archive/header_magic=] and the footer ([[#locating-an-archive]]).
  An archive without any metadata block decompresses into a single unnamed file rather than a directory tree.
  An [=overlay archive=] has its own media type `application/aqo` ([[#overlay-media-type]]);
  an encrypted archive whose payload is an overlay archive is also of that type.</p>
</dd>
<dt>Published specification</dt>
<dd>This document.</dd>
<dt>Applications that use this media type</dt>
<dd>Archivers and compressors that implement the Aqueous archive format.</dd>
<dt>Fragment identifier considerations</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Additional information</dt>
<dd>
  <dl>
  <dt>Deprecated alias names for this type</dt>
  <dd>N/A</dd>
  <dt>Magic number(s)</dt>
  <dd>F8 04 03 6A D6 BE (hexadecimal; [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=]) for an unencrypted archive,
  possibly preceded by other data;
  F9 CA F2 A6 D0 2A (hexadecimal; the common prefix of [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=]
  and [=const/ENCRYPTED_MIRRORED_HEADER_MAGIC=]) at offset 0 for an encrypted archive.</dd>
  <dt>File extension(s)</dt>
  <dd>.aqr<br>
  When a single file is compressed into an archive without any metadata block,
  the extension may be appended to the original file name instead (e.g. `example.tar.aqr`).</dd>
  <dt>Macintosh file type code(s)</dt>
  <dd>N/A</dd>
  </dl>
</dd>
<dt>Person & email address to contact for further information</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon &lt;public+aqr@mearie.org&gt;</dd>
<dt>Intended usage</dt>
<dd>COMMON</dd>
<dt>Restrictions on usage</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Author</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon</dd>
<dt>Change controller</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon</dd>
</dl>

<h3 id=overlay-media-type>
<code>application/aqo</code> Media Type</h3>

<p>This document requests the registration of a new media type in the Media Types Registry ([[!RFC6838]], section 5.6) as follows:</p>

<dl>
<dt>Type name</dt>
<dd>application</dd>
<dt>Subtype name</dt>
<dd>aqo</dd>
<dt>Required parameters</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Optional parameters</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Encoding considerations</dt>
<dd>binary</dd>
<dt>Security considerations</dt>
<dd>See [[#security-considerations]].</dd>
<dt>Interoperability considerations</dt>
<dd>
  <p>An overlay archive can't be interpreted without its base.
  Its magic numbers differ from those of `application/aqr`,
  so that a decompressor not supporting overlay archives doesn't mistake one for a standalone archive.
  The interoperability considerations for `application/aqr` otherwise apply.</p>
</dd>
<dt>Published specification</dt>
<dd>This document.</dd>
<dt>Applications that use this media type</dt>
<dd>Archivers, patching tools and updaters that implement the Aqueous overlay archive format.</dd>
<dt>Fragment identifier considerations</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Additional information</dt>
<dd>
  <dl>
  <dt>Deprecated alias names for this type</dt>
  <dd>N/A</dd>
  <dt>Magic number(s)</dt>
  <dd>69 0D 15 AF F6 B2 (hexadecimal; [=const/OVERLAY_HEADER_MAGIC=]) for an unencrypted overlay archive,
  possibly preceded by other data.
  An encrypted overlay archive has the same magic numbers as an encrypted archive of `application/aqr`.</dd>
  <dt>File extension(s)</dt>
  <dd>.aqo</dd>
  <dt>Macintosh file type code(s)</dt>
  <dd>N/A</dd>
  </dl>
</dd>
<dt>Person & email address to contact for further information</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon &lt;public+aqr@mearie.org&gt;</dd>
<dt>Intended usage</dt>
<dd>COMMON</dd>
<dt>Restrictions on usage</dt>
<dd>N/A</dd>
<dt>Author</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon</dd>
<dt>Change controller</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon</dd>
</dl>

<h3 id=structured-syntax-suffix>
<code>+aqr</code> Structured Syntax Suffix</h3>

<p>This document requests the registration of a new structured syntax suffix
in the Structured Syntax Suffix Registry ([[!RFC6838]], section 6.2) as follows:</p>

<dl>
<dt>Name</dt>
<dd>Aqueous Archive Format</dd>
<dt>+suffix</dt>
<dd>+aqr</dd>
<dt>References</dt>
<dd>This document.</dd>
<dt>Encoding considerations</dt>
<dd>binary</dd>
<dt>Interoperability considerations</dt>
<dd>
  <p>A media type with the `+aqr` suffix denotes a [=standalone archive=], either unencrypted or encrypted,
  whose [=archive value=] is given a more specific meaning by that media type.
  A generic Aqueous decompressor can [=interpret an archive=] of such media type without knowing that meaning.
  The interoperability considerations for `application/aqr` ([[#media-type]]) also apply.</p>
</dd>
<dt>Fragment identifier considerations</dt>
<dd>
  <p>The syntax and semantics of fragment identifiers specified for `+aqr` SHOULD be as specified for `application/aqr`.
  (At publication of this document, there is no fragment identification syntax defined for `application/aqr`.)</p>
  <p>The syntax and semantics for fragment identifiers for a specific `xxx/yyy+aqr` SHOULD be processed as follows:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>For cases defined in `+aqr`, where the fragment identifier resolves per the `+aqr` rules,
    then process as specified in `+aqr`.</li>
    <li>For cases defined in `+aqr`, where the fragment identifier does not resolve per the `+aqr` rules,
    then process as specified in `xxx/yyy+aqr`.</li>
    <li>For cases not defined in `+aqr`, then process as specified in `xxx/yyy+aqr`.</li>
  </ul>
</dd>
<dt>Security considerations</dt>
<dd>The security considerations in [[#security-considerations]] apply to any media type with the `+aqr` suffix.
Each such media type may have additional security considerations specific to its interpretation of the archive.</dd>
<dt>Contact</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon &lt;public+aqr@mearie.org&gt;</dd>
<dt>Author/Change controller</dt>
<dd>Kang Seonghoon</dd>
</dl>

<h3 id=etime-quantization-interval>
Quantization Interval</h3>

<p>This document requests the registration of a new key for the Time Tag Map Keys Registry ([[!RFC9581]], section 7.3) as follows:</p>

<dl>
<dt>Key Name</dt>
<dd><dfn>Quantization Interval</dfn></dd>
<dt>Key Value</dt>
<dd>-14 (tentative)</dd>
<dt>Reference</dt>
<dd>The [=Quantization Interval=] section in this document.</dd>
<dt>Semantics</dt>
<dd>
  <p>A quantization interval specifies the interval in which the true time lies, relative to the time value given.
  That is, if the time value is |t| and the quantization interval is from |a| to |b|,
  the true time lies between |t| + |a| and |t| + |b| inclusive.
  The quantization is any means to truncate or round the true time value to a discrete representation,
  and notably, is not related to the accuracy of the underlying clock.
  For example, it is possible that the clock has a high precision but the time values are truncated to the last second,
  in which case the quantization interval would be [0, 1 second].</p>
  <p>The interval is always closed.
  It may be slightly wider than the exact set of possible true times
  (e.g. [0, 1 second] for truncation, where the true time can never be exactly 1 second later),
  but it never excludes any possible true time, including ties in rounding.</p>
  <p>A quantization interval can be given in terms of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A numeric value in seconds or a duration |y|, indicating the interval of [0, |y|];</li>
    <li>An array of two elements `[x, y]` each of which is either a numeric value in seconds or a duration,
    indicating the interval of [&minus;|x|, |y|].
    That is, |x| is the extent before the time value and |y| is the extent after the time value.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Every numeric value MUST be finite and non-negative, and every duration MUST have a non-negative key 1,
  so that the time value given is always contained in the interval.
  The interval MUST NOT be a single point.
  Therefore a single numeric value or duration MUST NOT be zero,
  and an array `[x, y]` MUST NOT have both |x| and |y| zero.
  If |x| is zero, the array MUST NOT be used and |y| MUST be given alone instead.</p>
  <p>If an extended time doesn't contain this key, its quantization interval is unknown;
  in particular, the time value is not implied to be exact.</p>
  <p>For example, the first two of the following represent a time value truncated to 10 milliseconds,
  and the last one represents a time value rounded to the nearest second:</p>
<pre>
1001({1: 1697724754, -3: 870, -14: 0.01}),
1001({1: 1697724754, -3: 870, -14: {1: 0, -3: 10}}),
1001({1: 1697724754, -14: [0.5, 0.5]})
</pre>
</dd>

</dl>

<p>The following CDDL declarations can be used to represent the Quantization Interval key:</p>

<pre class=cddl>
quantization-interval = ~time / ~duration / [ 2*2 ~time/~duration ]
$$ETIME-ELECTIVE //= (-14: quantization-interval)
</pre>

<h3 id=dictionaries-registry>
Aqueous Dictionaries Registry</h3>

<p>This document requests IANA to create a new registry titled "Aqueous Dictionaries",
listing the [=registered dictionaries=] that a [=shorthand block=] of type 50<sub>16</sub> can refer to ([[#registered-blocks]]).
New entries are assigned with the Specification Required policy ([[RFC8126]], section 4.6).
Each entry has the following fields:</p>

<dl>
<dt>Hash</dt>
<dd>The [=registered hash=], i.e. the SHA-512/256 hash [[!FIPS180-4]] of the dictionary's bytes,
written as 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits.</dd>
<dt>Name</dt>
<dd>A short name of the dictionary for humans, which has no effect on the format.</dd>
<dt>Entropy Tables</dt>
<dd>Whether the dictionary has entropy tables, i.e. the value of [=dict/has_entropy_tables=].</dd>
<dt>Data</dt>
<dd>The bytes of the dictionary, being the [=decoded data=] of a dictionary block ([[#dictionary-block]]),
which MUST be valid as such and deposited with the registration.</dd>
<dt>Reference</dt>
<dd>The document describing the dictionary and its intended use.</dd>
</dl>

<p>The designated expert verifies that the hash matches the data and that no entry has the same hash.
An entry, once registered, MUST NOT be modified or removed,
since archives refer to it only by its hash.
An entry is never shared with the Aqueous Modules Registry ([[#modules-registry]]),
even when both have the same data.</p>

<p>The initial contents of the registry are as follows:</p>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Hash
      <th>Name
      <th>Entropy Tables
      <th>Reference
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><var>TBD</var>
      <td>text
      <td><var>TBD</var>
      <td>This document
    <tr>
      <td><var>TBD</var>
      <td>modules
      <td><var>TBD</var>
      <td>This document
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The dictionary "text" is intended for general text,
and the dictionary "modules" is intended for module blocks given in full ([[#module-block]]).</p>

Issue: The data and the hashes of the initial dictionaries are yet to be made.

<h3 id=modules-registry>
Aqueous Modules Registry</h3>

<p>This document requests IANA to create a new registry titled "Aqueous Modules",
listing the [=registered modules=] that a [=shorthand block=] of type 60<sub>16</sub> can refer to ([[#registered-blocks]]).
New entries are assigned with the Specification Required policy ([[RFC8126]], section 4.6).
Each entry has the following fields:</p>

<dl>
<dt>Hash</dt>
<dd>The [=registered hash=], i.e. the [=module hash=] of the module,
written as 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits.</dd>
<dt>Name</dt>
<dd>A short name of the module for humans, which has no effect on the format,
written as the name of the module and its version joined by `@` (e.g. `flate-set@1`).
Different versions of a module have the same name before `@` and different hashes.</dd>
<dt>Data</dt>
<dd>The bytes of the module, being [=module/wasm_module=] of a module block ([[#module-block]]),
which MUST be valid as such and deposited with the registration.</dd>
<dt>Reference</dt>
<dd>The document describing the transformation that the module reverses, its inputs, resources and parameters,
and preferably the source code from which the module was built.</dd>
</dl>

<p>The designated expert verifies that the hash matches the data, that no entry has the same hash,
and that the module conforms to [[#module-execution]].
An entry, once registered, MUST NOT be modified or removed,
since archives refer to it only by its hash.
An entry is never shared with the Aqueous Dictionaries Registry ([[#dictionaries-registry]]),
even when both have the same data.</p>

